// A shooter for as many machines as you like
// ==========================================
// Three dimensions, a mouse, and no server. Every node runs this same file, holds
// its own player and its own pack of the dead, and tells the others what both are
// doing; nothing in the mesh is in charge, and nothing is a client.
//
// **Alone it is you against the dead. With anybody else it is you against them and
// the dead**, and there is no switch between the two: playing alone is what it is
// called when the roster has one name in it. Somebody knocking on a single-player
// game turns it into a multiplayer one without either node doing anything about
// it, and them leaving turns it back.
//
// RUN IT — two terminals on one machine:
//
//   hive run fps.hive          listens on this machine's address, port 9200
//   hive run fps.hive 9201     the same address, port 9201
//
// The address is **not** written down anywhere in this file: it is asked of the
// machine with `hive.net.localAddress` (see `hereOn` at the bottom), so the
// endpoint a node hands out is one another machine can actually dial. Which is the
// whole difference between a game two terminals can play and a game two computers
// can. Both nodes print what they came up with on the way up.
//
// Who to join through is asked for on screen, filled in with the obvious guess —
// this machine, the other port — so on one machine you type nothing. A third and a
// fourth terminal need only a port of their own and somebody to join through,
// which may be *any* node already playing, since joining through one is joining
// through all of them (see "The mesh" below):
//
//   hive run fps.hive 9202 9200
//   hive run fps.hive 9203 9202
//
// That second argument only fills the lobby field in for you.
//
// TWO MACHINES need nothing else: start each one, read the endpoint it printed,
// and type that into the other's lobby. An argument with a colon in it is taken as
// the endpoint itself (`hive run fps.hive 10.0.0.4:9200`), which is what a node
// behind a router or on a chosen interface advertises. Every connection is TLS 1.3
// either way; the nodes only have to share a cluster key, generated into
// ~/.hive/syslink.key on first run — copy that file across, or set
// HIVE_SYSLINK_KEY the same in both.
//
// Or press "Play alone" and give nobody's address at all — the node still
// listens, so a second terminal can join the game you are already in.
//
// PLAYING: click the picture to take the mouse. W A S D walks, space jumps — and
// swims up — control dives, the mouse looks, the left button shoots, `t` talks, tab
// shows the score, escape gives the mouse back. Two bullets for a zombie and three
// for anything worse.
//
// THE PLACE is a hundred metres across, it is always night, and **no two games are
// on the same map**. The districts are always in the same quarters — a warehouse
// north-west, a stepped tower north-east, a market south-west, ruins south-east, a
// lake to the south, a walled yard in the middle — but how big each one is, what is
// inside it, how far the lake reaches, where the island in it stands and how high
// the walkway runs are rolled from a number when the game starts. Everybody in the
// game plays in the same map because the *number* travels, not the map; the world
// belongs to whoever's endpoint sorts first, which is a rule both ends of any pair
// work out alike without asking (see `plan`, `hash` and `settled`).
//
// The one district a seed never touches is the yard, and that is on purpose: a
// generated map wants one place everybody already knows.
//
// THE LAKE is deep enough to swim in and there is a dam across it. Its underside is
// level with the water, so a swimmer with their head out cannot pass it and a
// swimmer who dives can — and the island on the far side is either that dive or the
// long way round the shore. **Stay under too long and you drown**: a lungful is ten
// seconds, it comes back twice as fast as it goes, and the water starts on your
// health when it runs out. Being under it is also the only place on the map nobody
// can see you from, because the surface is drawn opaque from above and not at all
// from below.
//
// WHAT COMES FOR YOU is three things, and the fog gives you about a second's
// warning of any of them. **Zombies** walk at you and will not get their feet wet.
// **Mermen** never leave the lake and swim faster than you do. **Vampires** walk
// like a zombie until walking will not do, and then become bats and go over the top
// of whatever was in the way — walls, crates, the water — and every bite puts one
// back on its feet, so one you left at a sliver is whole when you meet it next.
// Each breed is kept up to its own number on its own clock, so a lake full of
// mermen says nothing about how many zombies are behind you.
//
// TALKING costs the mesh nothing. A line you type is one more message to the same
// address every position update goes to, down the connection that is already open
// to that node: `hive.syslink.on` hands back the live session rather than dialing a
// second one, so there is no chat server, no second port and no second socket
// anywhere. It arrives at the fold the keyboard arrives at, as one more variant.
//
// And it is typed **with the mouse still locked to the picture**, which is why it
// is not a text field. A field would have to be clicked, and the click belongs to
// the game; handing the mouse back to say hello and asking for it again afterwards
// is two clicks and a lost fight. `t` turns the keyboard into text and `enter`
// turns it back — one flag in the model, and `Pressed` is the only message either
// state needs.
//
// WHAT DOES THE WORK
// ------------------
// Three things, and the file is organised as the three of them:
//
//   * `hive.ui.scene` draws. A scene is a **value** — a camera and a vector of
//     shapes — rebuilt by an ordinary `func` from the state, sixty times a
//     second. Nothing here holds a mesh, a material or a canvas: `world` below
//     answers "what is there right now", the same way `view` answers "what does
//     the panel say right now". Night is part of that answer rather than a setting:
//     a dark sky, dark fog, and a palette to match.
//
//   * `hive.math` moves. Which way "forward" is, whether a bullet met anybody,
//     how far away they were, how a bat gets over a wall, how much of a breath is
//     left — all of it is arithmetic on the state, written in Hive, in `func`s a
//     test can call. **The map is arithmetic too**: `arena(seed)` is a `func`, so
//     the world is as testable as the walking, and two machines handed the same
//     number build the same boxes. None of it is in the browser.
//
//   * `hive.syslink` connects. Each node publishes its window under `#Arena`, so
//     "tell everyone where I am" is an ordinary send to an address, and being
//     told is one more variant arriving at the same fold as the keyboard. What is
//     said in the chat goes down that same connection, because there is only ever
//     one connection to a node — see `say`.
//
// THE PACK BELONGS TO SOMEBODY
// ----------------------------
// Every player carries a pack of their own — zombies, mermen and vampires, however
// many of each their own node rolled for — and **that node is the only one that
// moves them**. Everybody else draws them from what they are told, sixty times a
// second, exactly as they draw the other players. So the horde grows with the room,
// nobody is in charge of it, and a pack whose owner quits leaves with them — because
// nothing else was ever pretending to know where they were.
//
// It falls out of one rule, which is the same rule the players follow: the node
// that owns a thing is the only node that changes it. A shot at somebody else's
// zombie is a message to them, exactly as a shot at a player is a message to that
// player; a bite is a message from the pack's owner to whoever was bitten; and a
// shot at one of your own answers itself, in the same turn, with no message at
// all.
//
// THE MESH
// --------
// Nobody keeps a list of who is playing. A node knows one endpoint to start
// with — the one you typed — and asks it who *it* knows; every stranger that
// comes back is greeted in turn, and each greeting is answered with a roster too.
// So the introductions spread until everyone has met everyone, and a node that
// joins through any one player has joined through all of them.
//
// WHAT IS NOT GUARANTEED, AND WHY THAT IS THE HONEST ANSWER
// --------------------------------------------------------
// There is no referee, so each node is trusted with exactly the two facts only it
// can know: where its own player is, and whether it was hit. A shot is decided by
// the shooter (it has the aim), and the damage is applied by the target (it has
// the health) — which is why a kill is credited by the *victim*, in a `Killed`
// message, rather than claimed by the shooter. Two nodes can briefly disagree
// about a near miss, because each is drawing the other where it last heard it
// was. A tournament would need an authority; a game between friends does not, and
// this is what not having one costs.

import hive.ui
import hive.math
import hive.conv

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What crosses between the nodes
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// One mailbox, one type — the window's *and* the node's. A key you pressed and a
// player who moved on another machine arrive at the same fold and are told apart
// only by which variant they are.
type Msg {
	// From the screen.
	NameTyped { text: Str }
	PeerTyped { text: Str }
	Joined
	Alone
	Left
	Pressed { key: Str }
	Released { key: Str }
	Looked { dx: Float, dy: Float }
	Grabbed { on: Bool }
	Tick { ms: Int }

	// From the other nodes. `Hello` is the only one anybody waits for an answer
	// to, and `Here` is that answer — carrying the roster that grows the mesh,
	// whatever is loose in the arena so a newcomer arrives into the game already
	// in progress rather than into an empty one, and **the seed the world is made
	// of**, which is what stops the newcomer arriving into a different one.
	Hello { endpoint: Str, who: Str, seed: Int }
	Here {
		endpoint: Str
		who: Str
		roster: Str[dyn]
		horde: Fiend[dyn]
		seed: Int
	}
	Moved {
		endpoint: Str
		who: Str
		x: Float
		y: Float
		z: Float
		yaw: Float
		health: Int
	}
	// A shot somebody else fired, so every screen draws the same tracer.
	Saw { ax: Float, ay: Float, az: Float, bx: Float, by: Float, bz: Float }
	// "I shot you." The damage is applied by whoever receives this, because they
	// are the one holding that player's health.
	Hit { endpoint: Str, who: Str, damage: Int }
	// "You shot me." Sent by the player who went down, which makes it the one
	// claim about a kill that comes from the node entitled to make it.
	Killed { endpoint: Str, who: Str }
	Gone { endpoint: Str }
	// A line somebody typed. It is addressed exactly as a position is, so it goes
	// over the connection that is already carrying the positions — a node you can
	// see is a node you can talk to, and neither fact needed arranging.
	Said { endpoint: Str, who: Str, text: Str }

	// --- the pack ---
	//
	// Every node keeps a pack of its own (see "The pack"), and these are the four
	// messages that arrangement needs: what my pack is doing, that somebody shot one
	// of them, that one of mine bit you, and that one of yours finished off one of
	// mine. Each one follows the rule the players already follow — the node that owns
	// a thing is the only node that changes it. None of them says which *breed* did
	// anything, and none of them needs to: a bite carries its damage and a shot
	// carries an id, and the breed is the owner's business.
	Pack { endpoint: Str, horde: Fiend[dyn] }
	Clawed { endpoint: Str, id: Int, damage: Int }
	Bitten { damage: Int }
	Felled

	// From the greeter, which runs off the fold's thread.
	Met {
		endpoint: Str
		who: Str
		roster: Str[dyn]
		horde: Fiend[dyn]
		seed: Int
	}
	Unreachable { endpoint: Str }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The state
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

type Screen {
	Lobby
	Arena
}

// My own player. `y` is the ground under the feet, not the eye — the eye is a
// constant above it, and keeping the two apart is what makes standing on a crate
// mean anything.
type Body {
	x: Float
	y: Float
	z: Float
	fall: Float
	yaw: Float
	pitch: Float
	health: Int
}

// Somebody else, as last heard from. This is not a live handle on them: it is a
// message that arrived, and the picture is drawn from it.
type Foe {
	endpoint: Str
	who: Str
	x: Float
	y: Float
	z: Float
	yaw: Float
	health: Int
	score: Int
}

// Somebody one of the pack might go after. It is a flat little type on purpose:
// nothing hunting cares whether it is chasing this node's player or a player on
// another machine, and the code that steers it should not have to ask.
type Prey {
	endpoint: Str
	x: Float
	y: Float
	z: Float
}

// Somebody bitten, and what by. The damage says which breed did it rather than
// naming the breed — a merman's grab and a zombie's bite are not worth the same,
// and whoever receives it only needs the number.
type Bite {
	endpoint: Str
	damage: Int
}

// One step of the pack: where they all are now, and who they reached. The bites are
// handed back rather than sent, because working out what happened is a `func` and
// telling somebody about it is a `proc`.
type Assault {
	horde: Fiend[dyn]
	bitten: Bite[dyn]
}

// What a shot found. `Nobody` is a miss, and the two others say which of the two
// kinds of thing it was — because a player is told they were hit and the horde's
// keeper is told a zombie was, and those go to different nodes.
type Struck {
	Nobody
	Player { endpoint: Str }
	Undead { owner: Str, id: Int }
}

// A shot, briefly. `life` is how many milliseconds it still has to be seen for.
type Tracer {
	ax: Float
	ay: Float
	az: Float
	bx: Float
	by: Float
	bz: Float
	life: Int
}

// Something somebody said, and how much longer it stays on the screen. It carries
// the endpoint as well as the name because the endpoint is what a colour is
// computed from — the same colour that player's body has in the picture, so "the
// green one" means one person in the arena and in the chat.
type Line {
	endpoint: Str
	who: Str
	text: Str
	life: Int
}

// One solid thing in the arena. The same vector is what gets drawn, what a player
// bumps into and what a bullet stops at — one description, three uses, and no way
// for the picture to disagree with the collisions.
type Block {
	x: Float
	y: Float
	z: Float
	w: Float
	h: Float
	d: Float
	shade: Str
}

// Where somebody comes back after they go down.
type Spot {
	x: Float
	z: Float
	yaw: Float
}

// What a shot did. A pure `func` works this out and a `proc` sends it, which is
// the split that lets a test fire a gun without a network.
type Shot {
	at: Struck
	ax: Float
	ay: Float
	az: Float
	bx: Float
	by: Float
	bz: Float
}

type Model {
	me: Str
	mine: Str
	peer: Str
	screen: Screen
	aiming: Bool
	trouble: Str

	// The number the whole map is made out of. It is state like anything else, and
	// it travels: a node that joins somebody adopts theirs and rebuilds (see
	// `settled`), because two players in two worlds is not a game.
	seed: Int

	body: Body
	held: Str[dyn]
	others: Foe[dyn]
	tracers: Tracer[dyn]
	chat: Line[dyn]
	// This node's own pack, which it moves, and everybody else's, which it is
	// told about. Keeping them apart is what keeps the rule visible: one of these
	// is this program's to change and the other is not.
	pack: Fiend[dyn]
	swarm: Fiend[dyn]
	// One set of books per breed, which is what makes the lake filling up and the
	// yard filling up two separate things.
	broods: Brood[dyn]

	kills: Int
	felled: Int
	deaths: Int
	cooldown: Int
	down: Int
	// A lungful, and the clock between one mouthful of water and the next.
	air: Int
	gulp: Int
	beat: Int
	nextId: Int
	board: Bool
	// Whether the keyboard is text at the moment, and what it has typed so far.
	// The two of them are the whole of the chat's state, and the first one is the
	// only difference between a key that walks and a key that spells.
	composing: Bool
	saying: Str
	nodes: Str[dyn]
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The place, and where it came from
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A hundred metres of it, at night, and **no two games are on the same map**. The
// districts are always in the same quarters — a warehouse north-west, a stepped
// tower north-east, a market south-west, a field of ruins south-east, a lake to
// the south — but how big each one is, what is inside it, where the lake reaches,
// where the island in it stands and how high the walkway runs are all rolled from
// a number.
//
// So a game is a place you have to learn, and the quarter it is in is the only
// thing you knew before you arrived. The one exception is the walled yard in the
// middle: it is the same every game on purpose, because a map wants one thing
// everybody already knows.
//
// EVERYBODY IS IN THE SAME WORLD, which is the whole difficulty with a generated
// one and the reason none of this is `hive.crypto`. A map made out of randomness a
// node had its own opinion about would be a *different* map on the next machine,
// and two players would be shooting at each other through walls only one of them
// could see. So the map is a **`func` of one number**: same seed, same boxes, on
// every machine and in every test. The number is rolled once, by whoever started
// the game, and it travels with the introductions (see `Hello`, `Here` and
// `settled`) — a node that joins somebody rebuilds the world into theirs.
//
// All of it is boxes, including the ground. The same vector is what gets drawn,
// what a body bumps into and what a bullet stops at, so a stair you can walk up is
// a stair a bullet can be stopped by, and the floor being solid is what makes
// climbing out of the lake something you do rather than something that happens.

// A number out of two numbers. Knuth's multiplier, twice, and the sign thrown
// away: not good randomness, and it does not need to be — what it has to be is the
// *same* arithmetic everywhere, which is why it is written here rather than asked
// of the machine.
//
// `salt` is which decision is being made. Every call passes its own, so the
// tower's height and the lake's width are two independent rolls of one seed rather
// than one roll used twice.
func hash(seed: Int, salt: Int): Int {
	mut Int n = (seed % 2147483647 + salt * 7919) % 2147483647
	n = (n * 1103515245 + 12345) % 2147483647
	n = (n / 65536 * 1103515245 + salt * 40503 + 12345) % 2147483647
	if n < 0 {
		return 0 - n
	}
	return n
}

// A whole number from `low` to `high`, both ends included.
func between(seed: Int, salt: Int, low: Int, high: Int): Int {
	span := high - low + 1
	if span <= 0 {
		return low
	}
	return low + hash(seed, salt) % span
}

// A distance, to the tenth of a metre. Everything the map measures goes through
// here, which is what keeps the numbers in the plan below readable.
func spread(seed: Int, salt: Int, low: Float, high: Float): Float {
	steps := conv.round((high - low) * 10.0)
	return low + conv.itf(between(seed, salt, 0, steps)) / 10.0
}

// Whether something is there at all: `percent` times in a hundred.
func chance(seed: Int, salt: Int, percent: Int): Bool {
	return hash(seed, salt) % 100 < percent
}

// What a seed came to.
//
// Every loose decision about the map is made once, here, and the districts are
// built from the answers — so a district and everything that has to know where
// that district *is* (a spawn spot, a name in the corner of the screen, a merman
// wondering how far the water goes) are reading the same numbers instead of each
// rolling their own.
type Plan {
	seed: Int
	// The lake, the wall across it, and the island beyond that wall.
	lake: Pool
	dam: Float
	island: Spot
	// How high the walkway runs, which is also how high the loft it lands on is.
	deck: Float
	// The warehouse: where it stands, how big it is, and where its doorway is.
	shedX: Float
	shedZ: Float
	shedW: Float
	shedD: Float
	door: Float
	// The tower: where it stands, how wide its bottom tier is, and which side its
	// flights of steps climb.
	towerX: Float
	towerZ: Float
	tierW: Float
	side: Float
	// The market: where its first stall stands, and how far apart they are.
	stallX: Float
	stallZ: Float
	pitch: Float
	// The ruins: the middle of the field of them.
	ruinX: Float
	ruinZ: Float
}

func plan(seed: Int): Plan {
	// The lake first, because the floor is drawn around it and half the map wants
	// to know where its shore is.
	pond := Pool(
		spread(seed, 11, -2.0, 4.0),
		spread(seed, 12, 34.0, 39.0),
		spread(seed, 13, 28.0, 32.0),
		spread(seed, 14, 20.0, 25.0),
		spread(seed, 15, -4.2, -3.2)
	)
	mut Float which = 1.0
	if chance(seed, 44, 50) {
		which = 0.0 - 1.0
	}
	return Plan(
		seed,
		pond,
		// Across its northern third, so there is open water on both sides of it and
		// room for the island in the half beyond.
		pond.z + spread(seed, 16, -6.0, -3.0),
		// And the island beyond the dam, always: getting to it is either a dive or
		// the long way round the south shore. Where it sits is measured in fractions
		// of the lake rather than in metres, which is what keeps it off the shore
		// whatever size the lake came out.
		Spot(
			pond.x + pond.w * spread(seed, 17, 0.0 - 0.2, 0.2),
			pond.z + pond.d * spread(seed, 18, 0.18, 0.26),
			0.0
		),
		spread(seed, 21, 3.6, 4.4),
		spread(seed, 31, -34.0, -30.0),
		spread(seed, 32, -35.0, -31.0),
		spread(seed, 33, 24.0, 30.0),
		spread(seed, 34, 20.0, 25.0),
		spread(seed, 35, -5.0, 5.0),
		spread(seed, 41, 30.0, 34.0),
		spread(seed, 42, -34.0, -30.0),
		spread(seed, 43, 14.0, 17.0),
		which,
		spread(seed, 51, -49.0, -47.0),
		spread(seed, 52, 19.0, 21.0),
		spread(seed, 53, 7.6, 8.4),
		spread(seed, 61, 34.0, 38.0),
		spread(seed, 62, 32.0, 36.0)
	)
}

// How far out anything the game raises may go. The wall itself stands at
// fifty-five metres; the three between are its own thickness, a body's width, and
// enough room that nothing is ever raised with its back against it.
func edge(): Float {
	return 52.0
}

// Everything solid: the floor, and then everything standing on it. A body and a
// bullet are stopped by both, which is why both are here — `world` draws the two
// with different shapes, and that is the only place the difference shows.
func arena(seed: Int): Block[dyn] {
	made := plan(seed)
	return lawn(made) + built(made)
}

// Everything standing on the floor.
func built(made: Plan): Block[dyn] {
	walls := perimeter() + yard() + warehouse(made) + tower(made)
	rest := market(made) + ruins(made) + causeway(made)
	return walls + rest + shore(made) + scattered(made)
}

// The floor, as five boxes with a lake-shaped hole in it. Twenty-four metres of
// earth under each, which is not scenery: a swimmer who could pass through the
// bank from below would come out under the map, and this is the arithmetic that
// says they cannot. It is also why getting out of the water is something you do
// with the space bar — from down there the shore is a wall.
func lawn(made: Plan): Block[dyn] {
	pool := made.lake
	north := pool.z - pool.d / 2.0
	south := pool.z + pool.d / 2.0
	west := pool.x - pool.w / 2.0
	east := pool.x + pool.w / 2.0
	mut Block[dyn] floor = [
		Block(0.0, -12.0, (north - 58.0) / 2.0, 116.0, 24.0, north + 58.0, "#1b2a1d"),
		Block(0.0, -12.0, (south + 58.0) / 2.0, 116.0, 24.0, 58.0 - south, "#1b2a1d"),
		Block((west - 58.0) / 2.0, -12.0, pool.z, west + 58.0, 24.0, pool.d, "#1b2a1d"),
		Block((east + 58.0) / 2.0, -12.0, pool.z, 58.0 - east, 24.0, pool.d, "#1b2a1d"),
		// The bed of the lake, which is the one piece of floor that is not level
		// with the rest.
		Block(pool.x, pool.bed - 12.0, pool.z, pool.w, 24.0, pool.d, "#2a2a20")
	]
	return floor
}

// The four walls of the world, six metres of them. Nothing inside is tall enough
// to jump them from, which is the only reason they are that high.
func perimeter(): Block[dyn] {
	mut Block[dyn] walls = [
		Block(0.0, 3.0, -55.0, 112.0, 6.0, 1.0, "#2b2823"),
		Block(0.0, 3.0, 55.0, 112.0, 6.0, 1.0, "#2b2823"),
		Block(-55.0, 3.0, 0.0, 1.0, 6.0, 112.0, "#2b2823"),
		Block(55.0, 3.0, 0.0, 1.0, 6.0, 112.0, "#2b2823")
	]
	return walls
}

// The yard: the middle of the place, and the one district a seed does not touch.
//
// It was the whole arena once, back when the arena was forty metres square, and it
// is left exactly as it was for the reason a generated map most needs: somewhere
// that is the same every game. "Behind the bunker" and "the north gate" mean
// something to somebody who has never seen this map before, and everything else on
// this list has to be learned.
func yard(): Block[dyn] {
	mut Block[dyn] blocks = [
		Block(-12.0, 2.0, -20.0, 16.0, 4.0, 1.0, "#37301f"),
		Block(12.0, 2.0, -20.0, 16.0, 4.0, 1.0, "#37301f"),
		Block(-12.0, 2.0, 20.0, 16.0, 4.0, 1.0, "#37301f"),
		Block(12.0, 2.0, 20.0, 16.0, 4.0, 1.0, "#37301f"),
		Block(-20.0, 2.0, -12.0, 1.0, 4.0, 16.0, "#37301f"),
		Block(-20.0, 2.0, 12.0, 1.0, 4.0, 16.0, "#37301f"),
		Block(20.0, 2.0, -12.0, 1.0, 4.0, 16.0, "#37301f"),
		Block(20.0, 2.0, 12.0, 1.0, 4.0, 16.0, "#37301f"),

		// Cover in the middle, and a pair of towers to jump onto.
		Block(0.0, 1.0, 0.0, 6.0, 2.0, 6.0, "#3a434c"),
		Block(-8.0, 0.6, -6.0, 2.4, 1.2, 2.4, "#5c4022"),
		Block(8.0, 0.6, 6.0, 2.4, 1.2, 2.4, "#5c4022"),
		Block(-8.0, 0.6, 6.0, 2.4, 1.2, 2.4, "#5c4022"),
		Block(8.0, 0.6, -6.0, 2.4, 1.2, 2.4, "#5c4022"),
		Block(-13.0, 1.5, 0.0, 3.0, 3.0, 3.0, "#2f3841"),
		Block(13.0, 1.5, 0.0, 3.0, 3.0, 3.0, "#2f3841"),
		Block(0.0, 0.5, -12.0, 5.0, 1.0, 1.6, "#5c4022"),
		Block(0.0, 0.5, 12.0, 5.0, 1.0, 1.6, "#5c4022")
	]
	return blocks
}

// The warehouse, north-west: a shed with a door in its south wall, racks to work
// along, a gap in its east wall the walkway comes in through, and a loft at the
// walkway's height. How big it is, where its door is and how many racks it has are
// this seed's business.
//
// The crates are its inside stair, and the generator builds them as one: each is a
// jump higher than the last and the top one is a step below the loft, whatever
// height the loft came out at. `stair` is a `func` of the plan, so a test can walk
// up a stair it has never seen — see `fps.test.hive`.
func warehouse(made: Plan): Block[dyn] {
	west := made.shedX - made.shedW / 2.0
	east := made.shedX + made.shedW / 2.0
	north := made.shedZ - made.shedD / 2.0
	south := made.shedZ + made.shedD / 2.0
	doorX := made.shedX + made.door
	// The walkway comes in level with the loft, through the gap in the east wall.
	gap := made.towerZ
	mut Block[dyn] blocks = [
		Block(made.shedX, 2.5, north, made.shedW, 5.0, 1.0, "#33291f"),
		// The south wall, either side of the doorway.
		Block((west + doorX - 2.0) / 2.0, 2.5, south, doorX - 2.0 - west, 5.0, 1.0,
			"#33291f"),
		Block((doorX + 2.0 + east) / 2.0, 2.5, south, east - doorX - 2.0, 5.0, 1.0,
			"#33291f"),
		Block(west, 2.5, made.shedZ, 1.0, 5.0, made.shedD, "#33291f"),
		// The east wall, either side of the gap — which is a door at ground level
		// and the way in for the walkway four metres up.
		Block(east, 2.5, (north + gap - 2.2) / 2.0, 1.0, 5.0, gap - 2.2 - north,
			"#33291f"),
		Block(east, 2.5, (gap + 2.2 + south) / 2.0, 1.0, 5.0, south - gap - 2.2,
			"#33291f"),
		// The loft the walkway runs onto.
		Block(east - 7.5, made.deck - 0.2, gap, 15.0, 0.4, 8.0, "#3b2f20")
	]
	// Racks, across the end nobody floored over.
	for i := 0; i < between(made.seed, 36, 2, 4); i++ {
		z := north + 3.5 + conv.itf(i) * 4.5
		append(blocks, Block(made.shedX, 0.9, z, made.shedW - 8.0, 1.8, 2.0, "#3b2f20"))
	}
	return blocks + stair(made)
}

// The crates, climbing to the loft's edge. Each lid is a jump above the one below
// and the last is a step under the loft, so a stair is a stair whatever height the
// seed put the loft at — which is the sort of thing a generated map has to be able
// to promise.
func stair(made: Plan): Block[dyn] {
	east := made.shedX + made.shedW / 2.0
	rise := (made.deck - 0.35) / 4.0
	mut Block[dyn] crates = []
	// Westward from the loft's edge, each one lower than the last — so read the
	// other way it is four crates and then a floor.
	for i := 0; i < 4; i++ {
		lid := made.deck - 0.35 - rise * conv.itf(i)
		append(crates, Block(east - 15.5 - conv.itf(i) * 1.9, lid / 2.0, made.towerZ,
			2.4, lid, 2.4, "#5c4022"))
	}
	return crates
}

// The tower, north-east: three tiers and a mast, with a flight of steps up to each
// of them. It is the tallest thing in the place and the fog gives it up from
// further away than anything else, which makes it the one thing worth navigating
// by at night — and the walkway leaves from the top of it.
//
// Which side the steps climb is the seed's, so the way up is not the way up you
// learned last game.
func tower(made: Plan): Block[dyn] {
	first := made.deck / 3.0
	second := made.deck * 2.0 / 3.0
	mut Block[dyn] blocks = [
		Block(made.towerX, first / 2.0, made.towerZ, made.tierW, first, made.tierW,
			"#3c3a33"),
		Block(made.towerX, second / 2.0, made.towerZ, made.tierW - 5.0, second,
			made.tierW - 5.0, "#43413a"),
		Block(made.towerX, made.deck / 2.0, made.towerZ, made.tierW - 10.0, made.deck,
			made.tierW - 10.0, "#4a4841"),
		// The mast. It is the wrong colour for a building on purpose: this is what
		// you look for over the market's awnings when you are lost in the dark.
		Block(made.towerX, made.deck + 1.2, made.towerZ, 1.0, 2.4, 1.0, "#8e2c2c")
	]
	// Up from the ground on the south face, then along each tier to the next
	// flight. Going up a tower ought to be a walk rather than a series of hopeful
	// jumps, and a tread a third of a metre high is what that costs.
	foot := made.towerZ + made.tierW / 2.0 + 3.6
	up := steps(made.towerX, foot, 0.0, 0.0 - 1.1, treads(first), 0.0, 3.0, 1.1)
	mid := steps(made.towerX + (made.tierW / 2.0 - 1.3) * made.side,
		made.towerZ - 6.0, 0.0, 1.1, treads(second - first), first, 2.2, 1.1)
	top := steps(made.towerX + (made.tierW / 2.0 - 4.3) * made.side,
		made.towerZ - 6.0, 0.0, 1.1, treads(made.deck - second), second, 2.2, 1.1)
	return blocks + up + mid + top
}

// How many treads it takes to climb that far, and how much higher each one is than
// the one before it. The rise is under the height a player steps up without
// jumping — see the lid rule in `inside` — and that one number is the whole
// difference between a staircase and a wall with lumps in it.
func riser(): Float {
	return 0.32
}

func treads(climb: Float): Int {
	return conv.round(climb / riser())
}

// A flight of steps, as boxes. Each tread is a box from the ground up to its own
// lid rather than a plank on legs, so a flight is a stepped mass: nothing to see
// underneath and nothing to fall through.
//
// `dx` and `dz` are one tread's step along the ground, which is what says which
// way the flight climbs, and `base` is the lid it starts from — so a flight can
// begin on top of whatever the last one reached.
func steps(
	x: Float,
	z: Float,
	dx: Float,
	dz: Float,
	count: Int,
	base: Float,
	wide: Float,
	deep: Float
): Block[dyn] {
	mut Block[dyn] treads = []
	mut Float atX = x
	mut Float atZ = z
	for i := 0; i < count; i++ {
		lid := base + riser() * conv.itf(i + 1)
		append(treads, Block(atX, (base + lid) / 2.0, atZ, wide, lid - base, deep,
			"#454239"))
		atX = atX + dx
		atZ = atZ + dz
	}
	return treads
}

// The market, south-west: a grid of stalls, some of them low enough to shoot over
// and some of them not, in alleys wide enough for one person. How many of the
// sixteen places have a stall in them, how tall each one is and how far apart they
// stand are all the seed's — so the same corner of the map is a thicket one game
// and half empty the next.
func market(made: Plan): Block[dyn] {
	mut Block[dyn] stalls = []
	for i := 0; i < 4; i++ {
		for j := 0; j < 4; j++ {
			salt := 200 + i * 17 + j * 3
			if chance(made.seed, salt, 78) {
				x := made.stallX + conv.itf(i) * made.pitch
				z := made.stallZ + conv.itf(j) * made.pitch
				tall := spread(made.seed, salt + 1, 1.2, 2.8)
				append(stalls, Block(x, tall / 2.0, z, made.pitch - 3.4, tall,
					made.pitch - 3.4, canvas(made.seed, salt)))
			}
		}
	}
	return stalls
}

// An awning. Three of them, so an alley is somewhere you can tell you have been
// before — which is the only navigation a market gives you.
func canvas(seed: Int, salt: Int): Str {
	which := hash(seed, salt + 900) % 3
	if which == 0 {
		return "#5a2b28"
	}
	if which == 1 {
		return "#26405a"
	}
	return "#5e4c22"
}

// The ruins, south-east: walls that stop halfway along and pillars with nothing
// left on top of them. Every one of them is this seed's — how many, how long, how
// tall, and which way round they lie — which makes this the district that looks
// least like the one you played on last, and the one where you least often know
// what is behind you.
func ruins(made: Plan): Block[dyn] {
	mut Block[dyn] blocks = []
	for i := 0; i < between(made.seed, 70, 9, 14); i++ {
		salt := 300 + i * 11
		x := made.ruinX + spread(made.seed, salt, -13.0, 13.0)
		z := made.ruinZ + spread(made.seed, salt + 1, -14.0, 14.0)
		tall := spread(made.seed, salt + 2, 1.8, 4.0)
		long := spread(made.seed, salt + 3, 3.0, 12.0)
		if chance(made.seed, salt + 4, 22) {
			// A pillar: whatever is left of one.
			append(blocks, Block(x, tall / 2.0, z, 1.2, tall, 1.2, "#2f332e"))
		}
		if chance(made.seed, salt + 4, 22) == false {
			if chance(made.seed, salt + 5, 50) {
				append(blocks, Block(x, tall / 2.0, z, long, tall, 1.0, "#363b36"))
			}
			if chance(made.seed, salt + 5, 50) == false {
				append(blocks, Block(x, tall / 2.0, z, 1.0, tall, long, "#363b36"))
			}
		}
	}
	// One slab off one of them, lying where it fell. It is a step up and a place to
	// stand, which is more than the wall it came off is.
	append(blocks, Block(made.ruinX - 4.0, 0.4, made.ruinZ + 2.0, 6.0, 0.8, 6.0,
		"#41443e"))
	return blocks
}

// The walkway: from the top tier of the tower to the warehouse loft, straight over
// the yard's north gate and whatever is between them. Its height is the seed's,
// and everything that has to meet it — the loft, the tower's tiers, the crates
// inside the shed — is worked out from the same number.
//
// It is the one part of the place somebody has to show you, and the only way onto
// the loft that is not four crates. Being up there is worth something, which is
// the reason getting up there is awkward.
func causeway(made: Plan): Block[dyn] {
	from := made.shedX + made.shedW / 2.0
	to := made.towerX - (made.tierW - 10.0) / 2.0
	mut Block[dyn] blocks = [
		Block((from + to) / 2.0, made.deck - 0.2, made.towerZ, to - from, 0.4, 2.2,
			"#3a352f")
	]
	// Posts under it, at whatever spacing this one came out needing.
	mut Float at = from + 8.0
	for i := 0; i < 4; i++ {
		if at < to - 4.0 {
			append(blocks, Block(at, (made.deck - 0.4) / 2.0, made.towerZ, 0.8,
				made.deck - 0.4, 0.8, "#302d28"))
		}
		at = at + 12.0
	}
	return blocks
}

// The lake's furniture: the dam across it, and the island beyond the dam.
//
// The dam is the reason to dive. Its underside is level with the water, so a
// swimmer floating with their head out cannot pass it — the lid rule in `inside`
// is what says so, and nothing here is a special case for water. Getting under it
// costs a lungful, which is the whole of what makes the far half of the lake a
// place you have to earn.
// How wide the island's lowest tier is. Each tier above it is two metres narrower,
// which leaves a metre of rim to stand on all the way round — a sandbank you can
// only just get a foot onto is not one.
func sandbank(): Float {
	return 9.0
}

func shore(made: Plan): Block[dyn] {
	pool := made.lake
	mut Block[dyn] blocks = [
		Block(pool.x, 1.6, made.dam, pool.w + 2.0, 3.2, 4.0, "#2f3239")
	]
	// The island: a sandbank a swimmer can kick up onto, and three steps to a beacon
	// at the top of it. The lowest tier is *under* the water on purpose — anything
	// standing out of a lake is unclimbable from in it, because the height a body
	// steps up is measured from where the water holds it.
	mut Float lid = 0.0 - 0.15
	mut Float wide = sandbank()
	for i := 0; i < 4; i++ {
		append(blocks, Block(made.island.x, (pool.bed + lid) / 2.0, made.island.z,
			wide, lid - pool.bed, wide - 1.0, "#3a3527"))
		lid = lid + 0.3
		wide = wide - 2.2
	}
	// The beacon. It is the brightest thing in the map and it is on the far side of
	// the water: at night you can see where you are going long before you can see
	// how to get there.
	append(blocks, Block(made.island.x, lid + 1.6, made.island.z, 0.8, 3.2, 0.8,
		"#c9a44b"))
	return blocks
}

// What is left: cover in the open, scattered. Nothing lands in the yard, in the
// water, or close enough to a district to be its business — open ground with
// nothing in it is ground nobody crosses twice, and open ground with something in
// it every twelve metres is not open ground.
func scattered(made: Plan): Block[dyn] {
	mut Block[dyn] blocks = []
	for i := 0; i < between(made.seed, 80, 8, 14); i++ {
		salt := 400 + i * 13
		x := spread(made.seed, salt, -50.0, 50.0)
		z := spread(made.seed, salt + 1, -50.0, 50.0)
		if loose(made, x, z) {
			tall := spread(made.seed, salt + 2, 1.0, 2.6)
			wide := spread(made.seed, salt + 3, 2.0, 4.0)
			append(blocks, Block(x, tall / 2.0, z, wide, tall, wide, "#4a3a24"))
		}
	}
	return blocks
}

// Whether a place is open ground: outside the yard, out of the water, and not
// standing in a district's way.
func loose(made: Plan, x: Float, z: Float): Bool {
	if math.abs(x) < 23.0 && math.abs(z) < 23.0 {
		return false
	}
	if wet(made, x, z) || math.hypot(x - made.lake.x, z - made.lake.z) < 26.0 {
		return false
	}
	if math.hypot(x - made.shedX, z - made.shedZ) < 22.0 {
		return false
	}
	if math.hypot(x - made.towerX, z - made.towerZ) < 20.0 {
		return false
	}
	if math.hypot(x - made.stallX - 12.0, z - made.stallZ - 12.0) < 22.0 {
		return false
	}
	if math.hypot(x - made.ruinX, z - made.ruinZ) < 20.0 {
		return false
	}
	return true
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The water
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One lake, level with the ground, deep enough that you cannot touch the bottom
// and stand. Swimming is slower than walking, the space bar climbs, control dives,
// and **a head under the water runs out of air** — which is the price of the two
// things being under it is good for: getting past the dam, and not being seen from
// the bank.
//
// None of it is a special case anywhere else in the file. The floor is boxes and
// the lake is a hole in them; a swimmer is a body whose floor is three metres
// further down; the dam is a box whose underside is level with the water. What the
// water *is*, mechanically, is one rectangle and one depth.

// A body of water: where it is, how far it reaches, and how far down its bed is.
type Pool {
	x: Float
	z: Float
	w: Float
	d: Float
	bed: Float
}

// The top of the water is the top of the ground, which is what makes walking into
// a lake something you can do by accident.
func surface(): Float {
	return 0.0
}

// Where a body floats when it is not doing anything about it: eyes half a metre
// clear of the water, and the rest under.
func floating(): Float {
	return 0.0 - 1.1
}

// How fast you swim, and how fast you climb or sink while doing it.
func swim(): Float {
	return 3.2
}

func paddle(): Float {
	return 2.6
}

// A lungful, in milliseconds; what the water takes when it runs out, and how often
// it takes it. Ten seconds is long enough to get under the dam and back with
// something to spare, and short enough that the bottom of the lake is not
// somewhere to sit and think.
func breath(): Int {
	return 10000
}

func gasp(): Int {
	return 900
}

func drowned(): Int {
	return 14
}

func wet(made: Plan, x: Float, z: Float): Bool {
	pool := made.lake
	if math.abs(x - pool.x) > pool.w / 2.0 {
		return false
	}
	return math.abs(z - pool.z) <= pool.d / 2.0
}

func dry(made: Plan, x: Float, z: Float): Bool {
	return wet(made, x, z) == false
}

// Whether a body is swimming rather than walking: in the water, under the top of
// it, and with nothing under its feet. The third one is what keeps somebody
// standing on the island's sandbank out of the swimming rules — shallow water is
// somewhere you walk.
func swimming(model: Model): Bool {
	made := plan(model.seed)
	body := model.body
	if dry(made, body.x, body.z) {
		return false
	}
	if body.y > surface() - 0.35 {
		return false
	}
	return body.y > ledge(arena(model.seed), body.x, body.z, body.y) + 0.02
}

// Whether the water has closed over somebody's head. It is the eye that decides,
// because the eye is the thing the water closes over.
func under(model: Model): Bool {
	made := plan(model.seed)
	if dry(made, model.body.x, model.body.z) {
		return false
	}
	return model.body.y + eyes() < surface()
}

// The swim. It is `walked` with three differences and no fourth: slower, no
// gravity, and up and down are keys rather than something the world does to you.
func paddled(model: Model, dt: Float): Body {
	made := plan(model.seed)
	blocks := arena(model.seed)
	mut Body body = model.body
	step := heading(model, dt, swim())
	if step.dx != 0.0 || step.dz != 0.0 {
		if clear(blocks, body.x + step.dx, body.y, body.z) {
			body.x = body.x + step.dx
		}
		if clear(blocks, body.x, body.y, body.z + step.dz) {
			body.z = body.z + step.dz
		}
	}
	body.fall = 0.0
	// Where it wants to be: at the surface if it is climbing, at the bottom if it
	// is diving, and floating if it is doing neither.
	mut Float want = floating()
	if held(model, "space") {
		want = surface() - 0.4
	}
	if held(model, "ctrl") {
		want = made.lake.bed + 0.3
	}
	body.y = toward(body.y, want, paddle() * dt)
	return body
}

// A number moving toward another one, by no more than a step. Which is the whole
// of what "sinking" and "climbing" are, once gravity is not involved.
func toward(from: Float, to: Float, step: Float): Float {
	if to > from {
		return math.min(to, from + step)
	}
	return math.max(to, from - step)
}

// The air. It goes while the head is under and comes back twice as fast as it
// went, which is what makes surfacing for a moment worth the moment. When there is
// none left the water starts taking health instead, and going down to it is going
// down like any other — the count and the coming back are the ones every other
// death uses.
func breathing(model: Model, ms: Int): Model {
	mut Model next = model
	if under(model) == false {
		gained := model.air + ms * 2
		next.air = gained
		if gained > breath() {
			next.air = breath()
		}
		next.gulp = 0
		return next
	}
	next.air = floored(model.air - ms)
	if next.air > 0 {
		return next
	}
	next.gulp = model.gulp - ms
	if next.gulp <= 0 {
		next.gulp = gasp()
		return wounded(next, drowned())
	}
	return next
}

// Which district somebody is standing in, in words, for the corner of the screen
// and for the score board. A map you have to learn is a map worth being told your
// way around in, and on a generated one the answer has to come out of the plan
// rather than out of a list of rectangles somebody typed.
func whereabouts(model: Model): Str {
	return district(plan(model.seed), model.body.x, model.body.y, model.body.z)
}

// It takes three numbers rather than a body because the score board asks it about
// *other* players, and what this node has of them is the last thing they said they
// were — which is three numbers.
func district(made: Plan, x: Float, y: Float, z: Float): Str {
	if math.hypot(x - made.island.x, z - made.island.z) < 8.0 && y > 0.0 - 0.3 {
		return "the island"
	}
	if wet(made, x, z) {
		return "the lake"
	}
	if math.abs(x) <= 21.0 && math.abs(z) <= 21.0 {
		return "the yard"
	}
	if math.hypot(x - made.shedX, z - made.shedZ) < 20.0 {
		return "the warehouse"
	}
	if math.hypot(x - made.towerX, z - made.towerZ) < 18.0 {
		return "the tower"
	}
	if x < 0.0 && z > 15.0 {
		return "the market"
	}
	if x > 14.0 && z > 15.0 {
		return "the ruins"
	}
	if y > 3.0 {
		return "the causeway"
	}
	return "open ground"
}

// Which spot to start in: the one this node's port picks. Two players on one
// machine always differ in it, and it costs no round trip to agree on — there is
// nobody to ask when you are the first one here.
func mySpot(mine: Str, seed: Int): Spot {
	return spot(portOf(mine), seed)
}

// The port out of an "ip:port". A `Str` is what an endpoint is, so this is
// ordinary string work — and an endpoint that does not parse gets the first spot
// rather than an error, because where you start is not worth failing over.
func portOf(endpoint: Str): Int {
	parts := split(endpoint, ":")
	if parts bounds 1 {
		if conv.sti(parts[1]) is Result.Ok(port) {
			return port
		}
	}
	return 0
}

// How many places there are to start in. Eight rather than the yard's four,
// because a place this size is worth coming back into somewhere else: the walk
// back is a different walk every time, and half of them are a district you were
// not in when you went down.
func spots(): Int {
	return 8
}

// The eight of them: the yard's four corners, which are the same every game, and
// one outside each of the four districts that are not — read off the plan, facing
// in. `n` is taken modulo eight, so a port or an index may be handed in without
// either of them having to know how many there are.
func spot(n: Int, seed: Int): Spot {
	made := plan(seed)
	which := n % spots()
	if which == 0 {
		return Spot(-16.0, -16.0, 0.8)
	}
	if which == 1 {
		return Spot(16.0, -16.0, 2.4)
	}
	if which == 2 {
		return Spot(16.0, 16.0, 3.9)
	}
	if which == 3 {
		return Spot(-16.0, 16.0, 5.5)
	}
	// The warehouse door, and the foot of the tower's first flight — which is
	// where a player who has never been here starts, facing the way up.
	if which == 4 {
		return Spot(made.shedX + made.door, made.shedZ + made.shedD / 2.0 + 4.0, 0.0)
	}
	if which == 5 {
		return Spot(made.towerX, made.towerZ + made.tierW / 2.0 + 5.4, 0.0)
	}
	// The top of the market, and the edge of the ruins.
	if which == 6 {
		return Spot(made.stallX + 12.0, made.stallZ - 6.0, 3.14)
	}
	return Spot(made.ruinX - 16.0, made.ruinZ - 4.0, 4.71)
}

// Where to come back after going down: the nearest spot with room in it.
//
// On a place this size the *emptiest* spot is nearly always the far one, and a
// game that answered "the far one" would spend the next twenty seconds being a
// walk. It still has to have room — coming back on top of whoever put you down is
// not coming back — so what this asks is "the closest one nobody is standing in",
// which is two rules rather than one and worth the six lines it costs.
func vacancy(model: Model): Spot {
	mut Spot best = spot(0, model.seed)
	mut Float closest = 100000.0
	mut Bool found = false
	for i := 0; i < spots(); i++ {
		place := spot(i, model.seed)
		if lonely(prey(model), place) > 9.0 {
			gap := math.hypot(place.x - model.body.x, place.z - model.body.z)
			if gap < closest {
				closest = gap
				best = place
				found = true
			}
		}
	}
	if found {
		return best
	}
	return emptiest(model)
}

// The spot whose nearest living body is furthest away. It is what `vacancy` falls
// back to when there is somebody near every one of them, and it is where one of
// the pack is raised when a dozen guesses have all landed somewhere unfit (see
// `somewhere`).
func emptiest(model: Model): Spot {
	mut Spot best = spot(0, model.seed)
	mut Float room = 0.0 - 1.0
	for i := 0; i < spots(); i++ {
		place := spot(i, model.seed)
		nearest := lonely(prey(model), place)
		if nearest > room {
			room = nearest
			best = place
		}
	}
	return best
}

// How far the closest living body is from a spot — a very long way, when there is
// nobody about.
//
// It is handed the `prey` list rather than the roster, and that is the useful
// part: that list is exactly "everybody alive", this node's own player included
// when it is. So nothing is ever raised at a spot next to *me* either, and a
// player coming back is never in their own way, because somebody who is down is
// not prey.
func lonely(hunted: Prey[], place: Spot): Float {
	mut Float least = 1000.0
	for each one in hunted {
		gap := math.hypot(one.x - place.x, one.z - place.z)
		if gap < least {
			least = gap
		}
	}
	return least
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Moving
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Everything from here to "Shooting" is a `func` of the state. That is not
// tidiness: a `func` cannot act, so none of it can send a message or touch the
// screen by accident — and `fps.test.hive` walks a player into a wall without a
// window, a mouse or a second machine anywhere.

// How fast a player walks, in metres per second, and how hard the world pulls.
func speed(): Float {
	return 6.4
}

func gravity(): Float {
	return 20.0
}

// How high the eye is above the feet, and how wide a player is. The second one is
// used twice — to stop them walking through walls, and to work out whether a
// bullet found them — so it is written once.
func eyes(): Float {
	return 1.62
}

func girth(): Float {
	return 0.36
}

// One frame. Look and shoot arrive as their own messages; this is the part that
// happens whether or not anything did: the walking, the falling, the reload, and
// the tracers going out.
func stepped(model: Model, ms: Int): Model {
	dt := math.min(conv.itf(ms) / 1000.0, 0.05)
	mut Model next = model
	next.cooldown = floored(model.cooldown - ms)
	next.tracers = fading(model.tracers, ms)
	next.chat = hushed(model.chat, ms)
	next.beat = model.beat - ms

	if alive(model) == false {
		next.down = model.down - ms
		if next.down <= 0 {
			next.body = fresh(vacancy(model))
			next.air = breath()
		}
		return next
	}

	next.body = walked(model, dt)
	// The air is worked out from where the body *ended up*, which is the whole
	// reason this line is after that one: a frame that took you under the surface
	// is a frame you spent under it.
	return breathing(next, ms)
}

// Where a body ends up after `dt` seconds of whatever is held down.
//
// The two horizontal steps are taken one at a time, and each is given up
// separately if it would end inside something. That is what lets a player slide
// along a wall they are pushing into instead of stopping dead against it.
func walked(model: Model, dt: Float): Body {
	// In the water none of the rest of this applies: no gravity, no jumping, and
	// half the speed. It is the one branch, and it is here rather than in the fold
	// because whether you are swimming is a fact about where you are.
	if swimming(model) {
		return paddled(model, dt)
	}
	blocks := arena(model.seed)
	mut Body body = model.body

	step := heading(model, dt, speed())
	if step.dx != 0.0 || step.dz != 0.0 {
		if clear(blocks, body.x + step.dx, body.y, body.z) {
			body.x = body.x + step.dx
		}
		if clear(blocks, body.x, body.y, body.z + step.dz) {
			body.z = body.z + step.dz
		}
	}

	// Down, then what stopped it. A jump is a push upwards and nothing else; the
	// same three lines bring it back.
	body.fall = body.fall - gravity() * dt
	if held(model, "space") && grounded(model) {
		body.fall = 7.4
	}
	was := body.y
	body.y = body.y + body.fall * dt
	// The lid to land on is looked for across the whole step, not at the end of
	// it. Falling fast, a foot that was above a crate is below it by the time the
	// step is over — and asking "is there a lid near where I am now" would miss
	// it and drop the player through the crate.
	floor := ledge(blocks, body.x, body.z, math.max(was, body.y))
	if body.y <= floor {
		body.y = floor
		body.fall = 0.0
	}
	// A jump into the underside of something stops there rather than passing
	// through it.
	if body.fall > 0.0 && clear(blocks, body.x, body.y, body.z) == false {
		body.y = body.y - body.fall * dt
		body.fall = 0.0
	}
	return body
}

// A step across the ground: how far this frame goes and in which direction.
type Step {
	dx: Float
	dz: Float
}

// Which way the keys point, as that step. The yaw flattened, the diagonals
// normalised so that holding two of them turns you rather than launching you, and
// the whole thing scaled by whatever the medium allows — which is the only
// difference between walking and swimming, and the reason this is one `func` and
// not two.
func heading(model: Model, dt: Float, rate: Float): Step {
	ahead := axis(model, "w", "s")
	side := axis(model, "d", "a")
	if ahead == 0.0 && side == 0.0 {
		return Step(0.0, 0.0)
	}
	// Forward is where the eye is pointing, flattened: rotating (0, 0, -1) by the
	// yaw gives (-sin, -cos), and right is the same turn a quarter later.
	fx := 0.0 - math.sin(model.body.yaw)
	fz := 0.0 - math.cos(model.body.yaw)
	mut Float dx = fx * ahead + math.cos(model.body.yaw) * side
	mut Float dz = fz * ahead + (0.0 - math.sin(model.body.yaw)) * side
	length := math.hypot(dx, dz)
	if length > 0.0 {
		scale := rate * dt / length
		dx = dx * scale
		dz = dz * scale
	}
	return Step(dx, dz)
}

// Which way one pair of keys adds up to: forwards, backwards, or nowhere.
func axis(model: Model, plus: Str, minus: Str): Float {
	mut Float total = 0.0
	if held(model, plus) {
		total = total + 1.0
	}
	if held(model, minus) {
		total = total - 1.0
	}
	return total
}

func held(model: Model, key: Str): Bool {
	if indexOf(model.held, key) is Result.Ok(_) {
		return true
	}
	return false
}

func grounded(model: Model): Bool {
	floor := ledge(arena(model.seed), model.body.x, model.body.z, model.body.y)
	return model.body.y <= floor + 0.02
}

func alive(model: Model): Bool {
	return model.body.health > 0
}

// Whether a player standing at this spot is inside nothing. A block whose top is
// at or below the feet is not in the way — that is what makes a crate something
// you climb onto rather than something you bump into.
func clear(blocks: Block[], x: Float, y: Float, z: Float): Bool {
	for each block in blocks {
		if inside(block, x, y, z) {
			return false
		}
	}
	return true
}

func inside(block: Block, x: Float, y: Float, z: Float): Bool {
	if block.y + block.h / 2.0 <= y + 0.35 {
		return false
	}
	if x + girth() < block.x - block.w / 2.0 || x - girth() > block.x + block.w / 2.0 {
		return false
	}
	if z + girth() < block.z - block.d / 2.0 || z - girth() > block.z + block.d / 2.0 {
		return false
	}
	if y + eyes() < block.y - block.h / 2.0 || y > block.y + block.h / 2.0 {
		return false
	}
	return true
}

// The highest thing under the feet: the top of a block being stood on, or the
// ground. Only a top the feet are already at or above counts, so walking into the
// side of a crate does not lift anybody onto it.
func ledge(blocks: Block[], x: Float, z: Float, y: Float): Float {
	// Nothing under it at all. The ground is boxes like everything else (see
	// `lawn`), so "no floor here" is a real answer rather than an impossible one —
	// and it is a number rather than a special case because nothing inside the
	// walls can reach it.
	mut Float top = 0.0 - 60.0
	for each block in blocks {
		if over(block, x, z) {
			lid := block.y + block.h / 2.0
			if lid <= y + 0.35 && lid > top {
				top = lid
			}
		}
	}
	return top
}

func over(block: Block, x: Float, z: Float): Bool {
	if x + girth() < block.x - block.w / 2.0 || x - girth() > block.x + block.w / 2.0 {
		return false
	}
	if z + girth() < block.z - block.d / 2.0 || z - girth() > block.z + block.d / 2.0 {
		return false
	}
	return true
}

// Turning. The mouse reports how far it moved and the model decides what that is
// worth; pitch stops short of straight up and straight down, because there is
// nothing above the top of the sky to look at and rolling over would be worse.
func turned(model: Model, dx: Float, dy: Float): Body {
	mut Body body = model.body
	body.yaw = body.yaw - dx * 0.0024
	body.pitch = math.clamp(body.pitch - dy * 0.0024, 0.0 - 1.35, 1.35)
	return body
}

func fresh(spot: Spot): Body {
	return Body(spot.x, 0.0, spot.z, 0.0, spot.yaw, 0.0, 100)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The pack
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// **Every player brings their own monsters.** A node keeps a pack of its own —
// zombies on the ground, mermen in the lake, vampires that take to the air — steps
// them, raises them, and applies what people shoot at them; everybody else draws
// them from what they are told. So the horde grows with the room without anybody
// agreeing on anything, and no node is in charge of the game.
//
// That the *owner* simulates its own pack is the whole design, and it is the same
// rule the players already follow: the node that owns a thing is the only node that
// changes it. Two nodes stepping one zombie would disagree within a second, and
// "who shot it" would have no answer at all. Here a shot at one of the pack is a
// message to whoever owns it, exactly as a shot at a player is a message to that
// player — and a pack whose owner quits leaves with them, because nothing else was
// ever pretending to know where they were.
//
// It also settles what "single player" means: nothing. A node alone in the roster
// is fighting its own pack and nobody else's, and the moment somebody knocks the
// two packs are simply both there. No match to restart and no mode to switch.
//
// THREE BREEDS, ONE TYPE, and separate books for each. Which breed a thing is
// decides everything about how it moves and nothing about how it is owned, shot,
// sent or drawn — so there is one type with a kind in it rather than three types.
// A second type would have meant a second `Pack` message, a second vector in the
// model and a second copy of every rule above, to say what one field says.
//
// What is *not* shared is how many of each there are. Each breed is filled to its
// own number on its own clock, so a pack of five zombies and a lake with nothing in
// it is an ordinary night, and clearing the water does not slow the zombies down.

// The three of them.
//
//   * A **zombie** walks at whoever is nearest and never gets its feet wet, which
//     makes the lake the one place on the map they cannot follow you into.
//   * A **merman** never leaves the water, and swims faster than you do — which is
//     what makes the lake a risk rather than a refuge.
//   * A **vampire** walks like a zombie until walking will not do, and then becomes
//     a bat and goes over the top of whatever was in the way. Every bite puts it
//     back on its feet, so one you left at a sliver is whole when you meet it next.
type Breed {
	Zombie
	Merman
	Vampire
}

// One of the pack. `owner` is the node whose pack it belongs to — the only node
// that moves it, and the one a shot at it is reported to — and `id` is that node's
// own numbering. The pair is what makes "that one there" mean the same thing on two
// machines.
//
// `flying` is a vampire in the air. It is a fact about this one rather than a breed
// of its own, because the thing that lands is the thing that took off — and because
// a bat that had to be a different kind of thing would need its own `id`, and then
// shooting one halfway through the change would have no answer.
type Fiend {
	owner: Str
	id: Int
	breed: Breed
	x: Float
	y: Float
	z: Float
	yaw: Float
	health: Int
	bite: Int
	flying: Bool
}

// Every one of them anybody has, mine and everybody else's, as one vector — which
// is what gets drawn and what a bullet is checked against. `owner` is what tells
// the two apart, and it is where a shot is reported to.
func mob(model: Model): Fiend[dyn] {
	mut Fiend[dyn] all = []
	for each fiend in model.pack {
		append(all, fiend)
	}
	for each fiend in model.swarm {
		append(all, fiend)
	}
	return all
}

// Playing alone is not a mode this program has; it is a fact about the roster. A
// node with nobody else in it is fighting the pack only, and the moment somebody
// knocks it is not. Going back the other way is a `Gone` message.
func alone(model: Model): Bool {
	return len(model.others) == 0
}

// How fast each of them moves. Slower than a player on foot, every one — you can
// always outrun what is chasing you, and the trouble is never one of them. The
// merman is the exception that proves the rule: faster than a *swimmer*, which is
// the whole reason the water is dangerous.
func pace(breed: Breed): Float {
	if breed == Breed.Merman() {
		return 4.1
	}
	if breed == Breed.Vampire() {
		return 3.4
	}
	return 3.9
}

// A bat: quicker than the thing it turns into, still slower than a running player,
// and climbing about as fast as it flies.
func flap(): Float {
	return 5.4
}

func climb(): Float {
	return 3.4
}

// How high above its target one flies. High enough to clear the tower's tiers and
// the walkway, which is what "over the top of whatever was in the way" has to mean
// on this map.
func cruise(target: Prey): Float {
	return target.y + 4.2
}

func reach(breed: Breed): Float {
	if breed == Breed.Merman() {
		return 1.8
	}
	return 1.5
}

func teeth(breed: Breed): Int {
	if breed == Breed.Merman() {
		return 22
	}
	if breed == Breed.Vampire() {
		return 19
	}
	return 17
}

// What each is worth shooting: a zombie goes down in two bullets, a merman and a
// vampire in three — and a vampire that got a bite in between them does not.
func strength(breed: Breed): Int {
	if breed == Breed.Merman() {
		return 80
	}
	if breed == Breed.Vampire() {
		return 78
	}
	return 68
}

// What a bite is worth to a vampire.
func drink(): Int {
	return 18
}

// How high one starts: a merman floating, everything else on the ground it was
// raised on.
func perched(breed: Breed): Float {
	if breed == Breed.Merman() {
		return floating()
	}
	return 0.0
}

// Somebody one of them might go after: this node's player and every other one it
// has heard from, minus whoever is currently down. A pack belongs to one node but
// hunts *anybody* — which is what makes another player's monsters your problem too.
//
// It carries the height as well as the place, and that is not decoration: a bat
// works out how high to fly from it, and a swimmer three metres under the surface
// is out of reach of everything that cannot follow them down.
func prey(model: Model): Prey[dyn] {
	mut Prey[dyn] living = []
	if alive(model) {
		append(living, Prey(model.mine, model.body.x, model.body.y, model.body.z))
	}
	for each foe in model.others {
		if foe.health > 0 {
			append(living, Prey(foe.endpoint, foe.x, foe.y, foe.z))
		}
	}
	return living
}

// One step of this node's own pack: each of them goes after whoever is nearest and
// bites them if it can reach. The bites come back in a list rather than being sent,
// because this is a `func` — which is what lets a test stand a vampire next to a
// player and check that biting them healed it.
func stirred(model: Model, ms: Int): Assault {
	dt := math.min(conv.itf(ms) / 1000.0, 0.05)
	made := plan(model.seed)
	blocks := arena(model.seed)
	hunted := prey(model)
	mut Fiend[dyn] stepped = []
	mut Bite[dyn] bitten = []

	for each fiend in model.pack {
		mut Fiend next = fiend
		next.bite = floored(fiend.bite - ms)
		target := nearest(hunted, fiend.x, fiend.z)
		if target.endpoint != "" {
			toX := target.x - fiend.x
			toZ := target.z - fiend.z
			away := math.hypot(toX, toZ)
			// Facing is worked out even when it cannot move: something stuck behind
			// a crate still looks at what it wants.
			next.yaw = math.atan2(0.0 - toX, 0.0 - toZ)
			if bites(next, target, away) {
				next.bite = 900
				append(bitten, Bite(target.endpoint, teeth(fiend.breed)))
				next = fed(next)
			}
			// Something in the air moves whether or not it is over its target: it
			// has to come down to bite.
			if away > reach(fiend.breed) || next.flying {
				next = crept(next, target, made, blocks, dt)
			}
		}
		append(stepped, next)
	}
	return Assault(stepped, bitten)
}

// One of them, one step. This is the only place in the file where the three breeds
// differ from each other at all.
func crept(
	fiend: Fiend,
	target: Prey,
	made: Plan,
	blocks: Block[],
	dt: Float
): Fiend {
	if fiend.breed == Breed.Vampire() {
		return winged(fiend, target, made, blocks, dt)
	}
	return crawled(fiend, target, made, blocks, dt)
}

// A zombie or a merman: straight at whoever is nearest, one axis at a time so that
// it slides along whatever it cannot get through, and never out of the element it
// belongs in. The two are one walk with a different idea of where they may put
// their feet, and that difference is `belongs` and `rests` and nothing else.
func crawled(
	fiend: Fiend,
	target: Prey,
	made: Plan,
	blocks: Block[],
	dt: Float
): Fiend {
	mut Fiend next = fiend
	toX := target.x - fiend.x
	toZ := target.z - fiend.z
	away := math.hypot(toX, toZ)
	if away <= 0.0 {
		return next
	}
	step := pace(fiend.breed) * dt / away
	onX := fiend.x + toX * step
	onZ := fiend.z + toZ * step
	if belongs(made, fiend.breed, onX, fiend.z)
		&& clear(blocks, onX, fiend.y, fiend.z) {
		next.x = onX
	}
	if belongs(made, fiend.breed, next.x, onZ) && clear(blocks, next.x, fiend.y, onZ) {
		next.z = onZ
	}
	next.y = rests(made, blocks, fiend.breed, next.x, next.z, fiend.y)
	return next
}

// A vampire. On foot it walks like a zombie; the moment walking will not do — the
// step refused, the player standing somewhere above it, or too far off to be worth
// the walk — it becomes a bat, and a bat is checked against nothing at all. Walls,
// crates, the yard's gate, the lake: none of it is in the way of something in the
// air, which is the point of it.
//
// It lands when it is over its target and there is something under it to land on.
// It will not land in water: over the lake it hangs at biting height instead, which
// makes it the one thing that can reach a swimmer who has swum out of everything
// else's reach.
func winged(
	fiend: Fiend,
	target: Prey,
	made: Plan,
	blocks: Block[],
	dt: Float
): Fiend {
	mut Fiend next = fiend
	toX := target.x - fiend.x
	toZ := target.z - fiend.z
	away := math.hypot(toX, toZ)
	if fiend.flying {
		if away > 0.0 {
			step := flap() * dt / away
			next.x = fiend.x + toX * step
			next.z = fiend.z + toZ * step
		}
		// What it would land on, asked of the floor from above it — which is the
		// same question `walked` asks and the same answer.
		ground := ledge(blocks, next.x, next.z, cruise(target))
		if away < 2.4 && dry(made, next.x, next.z) && math.abs(target.y - ground) < 1.4 {
			next.flying = false
			next.y = ground
			return next
		}
		mut Float want = cruise(target)
		if away < 2.4 && wet(made, next.x, next.z) {
			want = floating() + 0.7
		}
		next.y = toward(fiend.y, want, climb() * dt)
		return next
	}
	afoot := crawled(fiend, target, made, blocks, dt)
	stuck := afoot.x == fiend.x && afoot.z == fiend.z
	if stuck || target.y > fiend.y + 1.4 || away > 20.0 {
		mut Fiend rising = afoot
		rising.flying = true
		return rising
	}
	return afoot
}

// Where a breed may put itself: a merman in the water and nothing else, everything
// else anywhere but.
func belongs(made: Plan, breed: Breed, x: Float, z: Float): Bool {
	if breed == Breed.Merman() {
		return wet(made, x, z)
	}
	return dry(made, x, z)
}

// Where its feet end up: a merman floats, and everything else stands on whatever
// the floor turned out to be there.
func rests(
	made: Plan,
	blocks: Block[],
	breed: Breed,
	x: Float,
	z: Float,
	y: Float
): Float {
	if breed == Breed.Merman() {
		return floating()
	}
	return ledge(blocks, x, z, y)
}

// Whether it can reach them: near enough across the ground, and near enough up and
// down. The second half is what a bat and a dive are both about — something
// hovering over your head cannot bite you, and neither can something floating at
// the surface when you are three metres under it.
func bites(fiend: Fiend, target: Prey, away: Float): Bool {
	if fiend.bite != 0 {
		return false
	}
	if away > reach(fiend.breed) {
		return false
	}
	return math.abs(target.y - fiend.y) < 1.8
}

// What a vampire gets out of it. This is the only thing in the game that puts
// health back into anything, and it is the reason a vampire is worth finishing
// rather than leaving: it drinks its way back to whole on somebody else if you do
// not.
func fed(fiend: Fiend): Fiend {
	if fiend.breed != Breed.Vampire() {
		return fiend
	}
	mut Fiend next = fiend
	next.health = fiend.health + drink()
	if next.health > strength(fiend.breed) {
		next.health = strength(fiend.breed)
	}
	return next
}

func nearest(hunted: Prey[], x: Float, z: Float): Prey {
	mut Prey closest = Prey("", 0.0, 0.0, 0.0)
	mut Float least = 1000.0
	for each one in hunted {
		gap := math.hypot(one.x - x, one.z - z)
		if gap < least {
			least = gap
			closest = one
		}
	}
	return closest
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Keeping the pack up to strength
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Three sets of books, one per breed. Each is four small questions and none of them
// needs a random number — which is what lets every one of them be a `func` a test
// can call. The fold asks them in order and does the two things that *are* impure:
// rolling how many to carry, and rolling where to raise the next one.

// One breed this node is carrying, and its own clock.
type Brood {
	breed: Breed
	want: Int
	sprout: Int
}

// The three a node starts with. The numbers are rolled by the fold the moment the
// game starts (see `arrived`) — a `func` cannot roll anything — and the clocks are
// staggered so that the first minute is not all of it at once.
func broods(): Brood[dyn] {
	mut Brood[dyn] all = [
		Brood(Breed.Zombie(), 1, 600),
		Brood(Breed.Merman(), 1, 2400),
		Brood(Breed.Vampire(), 1, 5200)
	]
	return all
}

func kinds(): Breed[dyn] {
	mut Breed[dyn] all = [Breed.Zombie(), Breed.Merman(), Breed.Vampire()]
	return all
}

// How long between one of a kind rising and the next of that kind, and how many of
// it a node may carry at the outside. A vampire is rare and slow to come back
// because one of them is most of a fight on its own.
func patience(breed: Breed): Int {
	if breed == Breed.Merman() {
		return 3200
	}
	if breed == Breed.Vampire() {
		return 6400
	}
	return 1600
}

func plenty(breed: Breed): Int {
	if breed == Breed.Merman() {
		return 3
	}
	if breed == Breed.Vampire() {
		return 2
	}
	return 5
}

// Every clock, one frame on.
func waiting(model: Model, ms: Int): Model {
	mut Model next = model
	next.broods = map(model.broods, counting(_, ms))
	return next
}

func counting(brood: Brood, ms: Int): Brood {
	return Brood(brood.breed, brood.want, brood.sprout - ms)
}

// Which breeds are owed one: the clock has run out and the pack is short of its
// number. Nothing here is a single answer, because two breeds may be owed one in
// the same frame and each is its own business.
func due(model: Model): Breed[dyn] {
	mut Breed[dyn] owing = []
	for each brood in model.broods {
		if brood.sprout <= 0 && counted(model.pack, brood.breed) < brood.want {
			append(owing, brood.breed)
		}
	}
	return owing
}

// And which are complete, which is when a new number is rolled for next time. So a
// player who clears their five zombies may find themselves with one, or five again,
// and never knows which — and what the lake is doing meanwhile is its own affair.
func filled(model: Model): Breed[dyn] {
	mut Breed[dyn] done = []
	for each brood in model.broods {
		if counted(model.pack, brood.breed) >= brood.want {
			append(done, brood.breed)
		}
	}
	return done
}

func counted(pack: Fiend[], breed: Breed): Int {
	mut Int total = 0
	for each fiend in pack {
		if fiend.breed == breed {
			total = total + 1
		}
	}
	return total
}

func wanted(model: Model, breed: Breed): Int {
	for each brood in model.broods {
		if brood.breed == breed {
			return brood.want
		}
	}
	return 0
}

func carrying(model: Model, breed: Breed, rolled: Int): Model {
	mut Model next = model
	next.broods = map(model.broods, wanting(_, breed, rolled))
	return next
}

func wanting(brood: Brood, breed: Breed, rolled: Int): Brood {
	if brood.breed != breed {
		return brood
	}
	return Brood(brood.breed, rolled, brood.sprout)
}

// One more of them, at a place the fold found. It arrives whole, already looking
// for somebody, and its own brood's clock starts again — the other two are not
// touched, which is the whole of what "separately" means here.
func raised(model: Model, breed: Breed, where: Spot): Model {
	mut Model next = model
	next.nextId = model.nextId + 1
	next.broods = map(model.broods, resting(_, breed))
	append(next.pack, Fiend(model.mine, next.nextId, breed, where.x, perched(breed),
		where.z, where.yaw, strength(breed), 0, false))
	return next
}

func resting(brood: Brood, breed: Breed): Brood {
	if brood.breed != breed {
		return brood
	}
	return Brood(brood.breed, brood.want, patience(breed))
}

// Whether a place is fit to raise a given breed in: inside the walls, in the right
// element, not inside anything solid, not on top of anybody — and near enough to
// somebody to matter.
//
// The last rule is the one a hundred-metre map needs. Something raised eighty
// metres away in an empty market is not a threat, it is a thing shuffling into a
// wall for the rest of the game. So what is wanted is a band: far enough that you
// hear it coming, close enough that it is coming for you. Which also settles what
// happens when everybody is down — nothing rises, because there is nobody for it to
// rise near. The pack follows the living about.
func decent(model: Model, breed: Breed, x: Float, z: Float): Bool {
	made := plan(model.seed)
	if math.abs(x) > edge() || math.abs(z) > edge() {
		return false
	}
	if belongs(made, breed, x, z) == false {
		return false
	}
	if clear(arena(model.seed), x, perched(breed), z) == false {
		return false
	}
	mut Bool near = false
	for each one in prey(model) {
		gap := math.hypot(one.x - x, one.z - z)
		if gap < shy(breed) {
			return false
		}
		if gap < earshot() {
			near = true
		}
	}
	return near
}

// How much room one wants when it rises. A vampire asks for more, because it covers
// the ground between faster than anything else here — and because arriving in the
// air is arriving from any direction at all.
func shy(breed: Breed): Float {
	if breed == Breed.Vampire() {
		return 15.0
	}
	return 9.0
}

func earshot(): Float {
	return 34.0
}

// What somebody shot, applied — and only ever by the node whose pack it is, which
// is what stops two nodes each deciding they killed the same thing. The fallen are
// dropped here rather than left lying: a pack is what is standing.
func clawed(model: Model, id: Int, hurt: Int): Model {
	mut Model next = model
	next.pack = standing(map(model.pack, wounding(_, id, hurt)))
	return next
}

func wounding(fiend: Fiend, id: Int, hurt: Int): Fiend {
	if fiend.id != id {
		return fiend
	}
	mut Fiend hit = fiend
	hit.health = floored(fiend.health - hurt)
	return hit
}

// Whether that shot is the one that finishes it. The owner asks *before* applying
// the damage, because "it was standing and now it is not" is the only moment worth
// telling the shooter about.
func finished(model: Model, id: Int, hurt: Int): Bool {
	for each fiend in model.pack {
		if fiend.id == id {
			return fiend.health > 0 && fiend.health - hurt <= 0
		}
	}
	return false
}

func standing(horde: Fiend[]): Fiend[dyn] {
	mut Fiend[dyn] left = []
	for each fiend in horde {
		if fiend.health > 0 {
			append(left, fiend)
		}
	}
	return left
}

// What another node just said its pack is doing. Their word replaces whatever we
// had of theirs and touches nothing else — the same rule as a player's position,
// for the same reason: they are the only node entitled to say.
func told(model: Model, endpoint: Str, pack: Fiend[]): Model {
	mut Model next = model
	mut Fiend[dyn] others = filter(model.swarm, notOwnedBy(_, endpoint))
	for each fiend in pack {
		append(others, fiend)
	}
	next.swarm = others
	return next
}

func notOwnedBy(fiend: Fiend, endpoint: Str): Bool {
	return fiend.owner != endpoint
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Shooting
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Where the bullet went and whom it found. Hit-scan: the shot arrives the instant
// it is fired, which is what every game of this kind does and what makes the
// answer a `func` of the state rather than something to simulate.
//
// The eye is the origin; the direction is the aim in full, pitch included. A
// player is a sphere around their chest, a wall is a box, and the nearest of the
// two is what the bullet met.
func aimed(model: Model): Shot {
	ox := model.body.x
	oy := model.body.y + eyes()
	oz := model.body.z
	flat := math.cos(model.body.pitch)
	dx := 0.0 - math.sin(model.body.yaw) * flat
	dy := math.sin(model.body.pitch)
	dz := 0.0 - math.cos(model.body.yaw) * flat

	// The wall is what the bullet meets if nothing else does, and everything else
	// has to be nearer than it to count. Players and the pack are checked the same
	// way and against the same distance, so whichever is in front is what is hit —
	// a zombie between you and somebody else is cover, and shooting it in the back
	// is a perfectly good idea. The floor is in that vector too, so a shot into the
	// ground stops in the ground.
	mut Float best = wall(arena(model.seed), ox, oy, oz, dx, dy, dz)
	mut Struck at = Struck.Nobody()
	for each foe in model.others {
		if foe.health > 0 {
			hit := struck(foe.x, foe.y, foe.z, ox, oy, oz, dx, dy, dz)
			if hit >= 0.0 && hit < best {
				best = hit
				at = Struck.Player(foe.endpoint)
			}
		}
	}
	for each fiend in mob(model) {
		hit := struck(fiend.x, fiend.y, fiend.z, ox, oy, oz, dx, dy, dz)
		if hit >= 0.0 && hit < best {
			best = hit
			at = Struck.Undead(fiend.owner, fiend.id)
		}
	}
	return Shot(at, ox, oy, oz, ox + dx * best, oy + dy * best, oz + dz * best)
}

// How far the shot travels before something solid stops it — or as far as anybody
// can see, when nothing does.
func wall(
	blocks: Block[],
	ox: Float,
	oy: Float,
	oz: Float,
	dx: Float,
	dy: Float,
	dz: Float
): Float {
	mut Float nearest = 90.0
	for each block in blocks {
		reach := entry(block, ox, oy, oz, dx, dy, dz)
		if reach >= 0.0 && reach < nearest {
			nearest = reach
		}
	}
	return nearest
}

// Where a ray enters a box, or a negative answer if it never does.
//
// The classic slab test: a box is three pairs of parallel planes, the ray is
// inside the box between entering the last of them and leaving the first. Written
// with `enters` and `exits` below so the three axes read the same.
func entry(
	block: Block,
	ox: Float,
	oy: Float,
	oz: Float,
	dx: Float,
	dy: Float,
	dz: Float
): Float {
	near := math.max(
		math.max(
			enters(ox, dx, block.x - block.w / 2.0, block.x + block.w / 2.0),
			enters(oy, dy, block.y - block.h / 2.0, block.y + block.h / 2.0)
		),
		enters(oz, dz, block.z - block.d / 2.0, block.z + block.d / 2.0)
	)
	far := math.min(
		math.min(
			exits(ox, dx, block.x - block.w / 2.0, block.x + block.w / 2.0),
			exits(oy, dy, block.y - block.h / 2.0, block.y + block.h / 2.0)
		),
		exits(oz, dz, block.z - block.d / 2.0, block.z + block.d / 2.0)
	)
	if far < near || far < 0.0 {
		return 0.0 - 1.0
	}
	return math.max(near, 0.0)
}

// Where the ray crosses into one slab, and where it crosses out. A ray going
// nowhere along an axis is either inside that slab for its whole length or
// outside it for the whole length, and the two absurd distances say which — which
// is what keeps a division by zero out of it.
func enters(o: Float, d: Float, low: Float, high: Float): Float {
	if math.abs(d) < 0.000001 {
		if o < low || o > high {
			return 1000000.0
		}
		return 0.0 - 1000000.0
	}
	return math.min((low - o) / d, (high - o) / d)
}

func exits(o: Float, d: Float, low: Float, high: Float): Float {
	if math.abs(d) < 0.000001 {
		if o < low || o > high {
			return 0.0 - 1000000.0
		}
		return 1000000.0
	}
	return math.max((low - o) / d, (high - o) / d)
}

// What a player is, to a bullet: one sphere around the chest, this high above
// their feet and this wide. It covers the body and the head, and it deliberately
// does not cover the legs — a shot at somebody's shins misses, which is a rule
// worth having on purpose rather than a rule that fell out of the arithmetic.
func chest(): Float {
	return 1.15
}

func build(): Float {
	return 0.7
}

// How far along the ray somebody was hit, or a negative answer for a miss. The
// nearest point on the ray to their chest, and whether that is within arm's
// length of it. It takes three numbers rather than a player because the living
// and the dead are shot at in exactly the same way.
func struck(
	atX: Float,
	atY: Float,
	atZ: Float,
	ox: Float,
	oy: Float,
	oz: Float,
	dx: Float,
	dy: Float,
	dz: Float
): Float {
	cx := atX - ox
	cy := atY + chest() - oy
	cz := atZ - oz
	along := cx * dx + cy * dy + cz * dz
	if along < 0.0 {
		return 0.0 - 1.0
	}
	offX := cx - dx * along
	offY := cy - dy * along
	offZ := cz - dz * along
	if math.sqrt(offX * offX + offY * offY + offZ * offZ) > build() {
		return 0.0 - 1.0
	}
	return along
}

// Bullets do 34, so three of them are enough and two are not.
func damage(): Int {
	return 34
}

// Milliseconds and health are `Int`s, and `hive.math` is `Float` throughout — so
// this is where the two meet, and it needs nothing from a library. Hive never
// widens a number behind your back (`hive.conv.itf` is how one crosses), which is
// what makes `math.max(0, health)` a compile error rather than a surprise.
func floored(n: Int): Int {
	if n < 0 {
		return 0
	}
	return n
}

func wounded(model: Model, hurt: Int): Model {
	mut Model next = model
	next.body.health = floored(model.body.health - hurt)
	if next.body.health == 0 {
		next.deaths = model.deaths + 1
		next.down = 2400
		next.held = nothing()
	}
	return next
}

func fading(tracers: Tracer[], ms: Int): Tracer[dyn] {
	mut Tracer[dyn] left = []
	for each tracer in tracers {
		if tracer.life > ms {
			append(left, Tracer(tracer.ax, tracer.ay, tracer.az, tracer.bx,
				tracer.by, tracer.bz, tracer.life - ms))
		}
	}
	return left
}

func tracing(shot: Shot): Tracer {
	return Tracer(shot.ax, shot.ay, shot.az, shot.bx, shot.by, shot.bz, 120)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Hearing from the others
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

func met(model: Model, endpoint: Str, who: Str): Model {
	mut Model next = model
	if endpoint == model.mine {
		return next
	}
	if knows(model.others, endpoint) {
		next.others = map(model.others, renamed(_, endpoint, who))
		return next
	}
	append(next.others, Foe(endpoint, who, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 100, 0))
	return next
}

// Somebody moved. This replaces what we had for them rather than adding to it —
// the message *is* their position, and the last one to arrive is the truth.
func moved(model: Model, msg: Msg): Model {
	if msg is Msg.Moved(endpoint, who, x, y, z, yaw, health) {
		mut Model next = met(model, endpoint, who)
		next.others = map(next.others, placed(_, endpoint, who, x, y, z, yaw, health))
		return next
	}
	return model
}

func placed(
	foe: Foe,
	endpoint: Str,
	who: Str,
	x: Float,
	y: Float,
	z: Float,
	yaw: Float,
	health: Int
): Foe {
	if foe.endpoint != endpoint {
		return foe
	}
	return Foe(endpoint, who, x, y, z, yaw, health, foe.score)
}

func credited(foe: Foe, endpoint: Str): Foe {
	if foe.endpoint != endpoint {
		return foe
	}
	return Foe(foe.endpoint, foe.who, foe.x, foe.y, foe.z, foe.yaw, foe.health,
		foe.score + 1)
}

func renamed(foe: Foe, endpoint: Str, who: Str): Foe {
	if foe.endpoint != endpoint {
		return foe
	}
	return Foe(endpoint, who, foe.x, foe.y, foe.z, foe.yaw, foe.health, foe.score)
}

func parted(model: Model, endpoint: Str): Model {
	mut Model next = model
	next.others = filter(model.others, isNot(_, endpoint))
	return next
}

func isNot(foe: Foe, endpoint: Str): Bool {
	return foe.endpoint != endpoint
}

func knows(others: Foe[], endpoint: Str): Bool {
	for each foe in others {
		if foe.endpoint == endpoint {
			return true
		}
	}
	return false
}

func endpointOf(foe: Foe): Str {
	return foe.endpoint
}

// Everybody this node has met, itself included — the answer a `Hello` comes back
// with, and the reason joining through one player is joining through all of them.
func roster(model: Model): Str[dyn] {
	mut Str[dyn] all = [model.mine]
	for each endpoint in map(model.others, endpointOf) {
		append(all, endpoint)
	}
	return all
}

// Which of a roster are worth greeting: not me, and not somebody already known.
func strangers(model: Model, heard: Str[]): Str[dyn] {
	mut Str[dyn] fresh = []
	for each endpoint in heard {
		if endpoint != model.mine && knows(model.others, endpoint) == false {
			append(fresh, endpoint)
		}
	}
	return fresh
}

func usable(name: Str): Bool {
	return join(split(name, " "), "") != ""
}

func ready(model: Model): Bool {
	return usable(model.me) && usable(model.peer)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Talking
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A chat, and **not one made out of `ui.input`** — which is worth saying why,
// because a text field is the obvious answer and it is the wrong one here. A field
// has to be clicked to be typed into, and the mouse is not the player's to click
// with: it is locked to the picture, which is what makes looking around work.
//
// So the line is composed out of the keyboard the game is already reading. A key
// arrives as `Pressed` whether it means "walk forward" or means the letter `w`, and
// which of the two it means is one flag in the model. That is the whole mechanism,
// and everything under it is a `func` a test can call — so `fps.test.hive` holds a
// conversation with no keyboard and no second machine in it.
//
// What it costs is capitals: a key is reported by the name of the key, and that
// name is `a` whether or not shift was held. Everybody shouting in lower case
// seemed a fair price for never letting go of the mouse.

// How long a line stays on the screen, and how many are kept. Both are small: a
// chat that scrolled would be a page, and this is a game.
func chatting(): Int {
	return 14000
}

func remembered(): Int {
	return 5
}

// The longest thing anybody may say. It is applied to what arrives as well as to
// what is typed, because how much of the screen a line may take is this screen's
// business and the node that sent it is not the one drawing it.
func mouthful(): Int {
	return 90
}

// Starting a line. Whatever was held goes with it: a player who was running when
// they started typing would otherwise run into a wall for as long as the sentence
// took, and none of those keys is a key any more — they are letters now.
func opening(model: Model): Model {
	mut Model next = model
	next.composing = true
	next.saying = ""
	next.held = nothing()
	return next
}

// Done with it, either way. Sending is what `say` does; this is only the part
// where the keyboard goes back to being a keyboard.
func sealed(model: Model): Model {
	mut Model next = model
	next.composing = false
	next.saying = ""
	return next
}

// One key into the line. A letter, a digit or a punctuation mark is itself — that
// is what the renderer reports — space is a space, backspace takes one back, and
// everything else (shift, tab, the arrows, a mouse button) is not text and is
// ignored. Which is the whole of the difference: one character long means type it.
func typed(model: Model, key: Str): Model {
	mut Model next = model
	if key == "space" {
		next.saying = clipped("{model.saying} ")
		return next
	}
	if key == "backspace" {
		next.saying = rubbed(model.saying)
		return next
	}
	if len(split(key, "")) == 1 {
		next.saying = clipped("{model.saying}{key}")
	}
	return next
}

// Every character but the last. `for each` never indexes, which is why this is
// four lines and not a bounds proof.
func rubbed(text: Str): Str {
	letters := split(text, "")
	mut Str[dyn] left = []
	mut Int seen = 0
	for each letter in letters {
		seen = seen + 1
		if seen < len(letters) {
			append(left, letter)
		}
	}
	return join(left, "")
}

// At most a mouthful of it.
func clipped(text: Str): Str {
	mut Str[dyn] kept = []
	mut Int seen = 0
	for each letter in split(text, "") {
		seen = seen + 1
		if seen <= mouthful() {
			append(kept, letter)
		}
	}
	return join(kept, "")
}

// The log with one more line at the end of it, and the oldest dropped when that
// makes it too long.
func noted(lines: Line[], endpoint: Str, who: Str, text: Str): Line[dyn] {
	over := len(lines) + 1 - remembered()
	mut Line[dyn] said = []
	mut Int seen = 0
	for each line in lines {
		if seen >= over {
			append(said, line)
		}
		seen = seen + 1
	}
	append(said, Line(endpoint, who, clipped(text), chatting()))
	return said
}

// Somebody said something. Hearing a stranger speak introduces them, exactly as
// hearing one move does: a line can arrive before the introduction it belongs to,
// and the mesh is meant not to care what order things turn up in.
func spoke(model: Model, endpoint: Str, who: Str, text: Str): Model {
	mut Model next = met(model, endpoint, who)
	next.chat = noted(model.chat, endpoint, who, text)
	return next
}

// Lines going quiet, the same way tracers fade — and for the same reason: what is
// on the screen is what is recent, and nothing has to be cleared away.
func hushed(lines: Line[], ms: Int): Line[dyn] {
	mut Line[dyn] left = []
	for each line in lines {
		if line.life > ms {
			append(left, Line(line.endpoint, line.who, line.text, line.life - ms))
		}
	}
	return left
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The picture
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A scene is a value, so this is a `func` like any other view — and, like any
// other view, it cannot act. Sixty of these are built a second and thrown away;
// if drawing could send a message, every frame would send it again.
func world(model: Model): ui.Shape[dyn] {
	made := plan(model.seed)
	mut ui.Shape[dyn] shapes = []
	// The floor. It is the same boxes the collisions use — one description, two
	// uses — drawn as `ground` planes rather than as boxes because `ground` is the
	// shape the renderer puts grass on, and a floor wants grass.
	for each slab in lawn(made) {
		append(shapes, ui.ground(
			[
				ui.at(slab.x, slab.y + slab.h / 2.0, slab.z),
				ui.paint(ui.Tone.HEX(slab.shade))
			],
			slab.w,
			slab.d
		))
	}
	// The water, which is the one thing drawn that nothing collides with: you swim
	// through it. A box rather than a plane, and that is worth a word — a box has an
	// underside, so from the bank the lake is opaque and from below it is not there
	// at all. Which is exactly what a dive is worth: they cannot see you, and you
	// can see them.
	append(shapes, ui.box(
		[
			ui.at(made.lake.x, surface() - 0.06, made.lake.z),
			ui.paint(ui.Tone.HEX("#12283d"))
		],
		made.lake.w,
		0.12,
		made.lake.d
	))
	for each block in built(made) {
		append(shapes, ui.box(
			[
				ui.at(block.x, block.y, block.z),
				ui.paint(ui.Tone.HEX(block.shade))
			],
			block.w,
			block.h,
			block.d
		))
	}
	for each foe in model.others {
		for each piece in player(foe) {
			append(shapes, piece)
		}
	}
	// Every pack in the arena, mine and everybody else's, drawn the same way —
	// which is the point of `mob`: from here they are all just things prowling
	// about, and whose they are only matters when one is shot.
	for each fiend in mob(model) {
		for each piece in beast(fiend) {
			append(shapes, piece)
		}
	}
	for each tracer in model.tracers {
		append(shapes, ui.line(
			[ui.paint(ui.Tone.HEX("#ffe066"))],
			tracer.ax,
			tracer.ay,
			tracer.az,
			tracer.bx,
			tracer.by,
			tracer.bz
		))
	}
	return shapes
}

// Somebody else: a body, a head, a snout to show which way they are facing, and
// their name above them. Four shapes and a colour computed from their endpoint —
// which is what `Tone.HEX` is for, since there is no role called "the other
// player" and no closed set could have one.
func player(foe: Foe): ui.Shape[dyn] {
	mut ui.Shape[dyn] pieces = []
	if foe.health <= 0 {
		// Down, and lying where they fell rather than gone: a game that hides its
		// dead makes the two seconds before they are back read as a disconnection.
		append(pieces, ui.box(
			[ui.at(foe.x, foe.y + 0.15, foe.z), ui.paint(ui.Tone.Muted())],
			1.2,
			0.3,
			0.6
		))
		return pieces
	}
	shade := colour(foe.endpoint)
	append(pieces, ui.box(
		[
			ui.at(foe.x, foe.y + 0.62, foe.z),
			ui.turn(0.0, foe.yaw, 0.0),
			ui.paint(shade)
		],
		0.62,
		1.24,
		0.42
	))
	append(pieces, ui.sphere(
		[ui.at(foe.x, foe.y + 1.48, foe.z), ui.paint(ui.Tone.HEX("#f2d3ae"))],
		0.26
	))
	// Where they are looking, so a player about to be shot at can tell.
	append(pieces, ui.box(
		[
			ui.at(
				foe.x - math.sin(foe.yaw) * 0.42,
				foe.y + 1.2,
				foe.z - math.cos(foe.yaw) * 0.42
			),
			ui.turn(0.0, foe.yaw, 0.0),
			ui.paint(ui.Tone.HEX("#2b2f36"))
		],
		0.12,
		0.12,
		0.7
	))
	append(pieces, ui.label(
		[ui.at(foe.x, foe.y + 2.1, foe.z), ui.paint(ui.Tone.HEX("#ffffff"))],
		"{foe.who}  {foe.health}"
	))
	return pieces
}

// One of the pack, whichever it is. The three of them have to be told apart at
// forty metres in the dark, so the difference is shape before it is colour: a
// zombie has its arms out, a merman is a head and shoulders above the water and
// nothing else, and a bat is wings.
func beast(fiend: Fiend): ui.Shape[dyn] {
	if fiend.breed == Breed.Merman() {
		return swimmer(fiend)
	}
	if fiend.breed == Breed.Vampire() {
		return stalker(fiend)
	}
	return walker(fiend)
}

// A merman: a hunched back and a head at the waterline, with a fin to either side.
// Most of it is under the water and the water is drawn opaque, so what you actually
// see is a shape moving at you and no legs — which is the whole idea.
func swimmer(fiend: Fiend): ui.Shape[dyn] {
	skin := scale(fiend.health)
	mut ui.Shape[dyn] pieces = [
		ui.box(
			[
				ui.at(fiend.x, fiend.y + 1.0, fiend.z),
				ui.turn(0.0, fiend.yaw, 0.0),
				ui.paint(skin)
			],
			0.62,
			0.9,
			0.5
		),
		ui.sphere(
			[ui.at(fiend.x, fiend.y + 1.6, fiend.z), ui.paint(skin)],
			0.26
		)
	]
	for each side in [0.0 - 1.0, 1.0] {
		append(pieces, ui.box(
			[
				ui.at(
					fiend.x + math.cos(fiend.yaw) * 0.42 * side,
					fiend.y + 1.05,
					fiend.z - math.sin(fiend.yaw) * 0.42 * side
				),
				ui.turn(0.0, fiend.yaw, 0.0),
				ui.paint(skin)
			],
			0.5,
			0.1,
			0.28
		))
	}
	return pieces
}

// A vampire, on its feet or in the air. On the ground it is tall and dark with a
// pale head and a cape; in the air it is a body a third the size and two wings, and
// nothing else — because a bat coming at you over a wall has to read as a bat in
// the half second you have to shoot it in.
func stalker(fiend: Fiend): ui.Shape[dyn] {
	skin := pallor(fiend.health)
	if fiend.flying {
		mut ui.Shape[dyn] wings = [
			ui.box(
				[
					ui.at(fiend.x, fiend.y + 0.9, fiend.z),
					ui.turn(0.0, fiend.yaw, 0.0),
					ui.paint(skin)
				],
				0.3,
				0.42,
				0.3
			)
		]
		for each side in [0.0 - 1.0, 1.0] {
			append(wings, ui.box(
				[
					ui.at(
						fiend.x + math.cos(fiend.yaw) * 0.62 * side,
						fiend.y + 1.05,
						fiend.z - math.sin(fiend.yaw) * 0.62 * side
					),
					ui.turn(0.0, fiend.yaw, 0.35 * side),
					ui.paint(skin)
				],
				1.1,
				0.08,
				0.6
			))
		}
		return wings
	}
	mut ui.Shape[dyn] pieces = [
		ui.box(
			[
				ui.at(fiend.x, fiend.y + 0.72, fiend.z),
				ui.turn(0.0, fiend.yaw, 0.0),
				ui.paint(skin)
			],
			0.5,
			1.44,
			0.36
		),
		ui.sphere(
			[
				ui.at(fiend.x, fiend.y + 1.64, fiend.z),
				ui.paint(ui.Tone.HEX("#c8bfae"))
			],
			0.24
		),
		// The cape, which is the whole of what makes it read as a vampire rather
		// than as a thin zombie.
		ui.box(
			[
				ui.at(
					fiend.x + math.sin(fiend.yaw) * 0.26,
					fiend.y + 0.86,
					fiend.z + math.cos(fiend.yaw) * 0.26
				),
				ui.turn(0.0, fiend.yaw, 0.0),
				ui.paint(ui.Tone.HEX("#20161c"))
			],
			1.0,
			1.2,
			0.12
		)
	]
	return pieces
}

// A zombie: a hunched body, a head, and two arms held out in front — which is the
// whole of what makes a box read as a zombie rather than as a crate. They go dull
// as they are shot, so how much fight is left in one is something you can see
// rather than something you have to remember.
func walker(fiend: Fiend): ui.Shape[dyn] {
	zombie := fiend
	skin := rot(zombie.health)
	mut ui.Shape[dyn] pieces = [
		ui.box(
			[
				ui.at(zombie.x, zombie.y + 0.6, zombie.z),
				ui.turn(0.0, zombie.yaw, 0.0),
				ui.paint(skin)
			],
			0.56,
			1.2,
			0.38
		),
		ui.sphere(
			[ui.at(zombie.x, zombie.y + 1.42, zombie.z), ui.paint(skin)],
			0.25
		)
	]
	// The arms, reaching. Both are the same box moved out to either side of
	// wherever the thing is facing, which is two lines of trigonometry and the
	// only reason a zombie needs any.
	across := 0.26
	ahead := 0.34
	for each side in [0.0 - 1.0, 1.0] {
		append(pieces, ui.box(
			[
				ui.at(
					zombie.x - math.sin(zombie.yaw) * ahead + math.cos(zombie.yaw) * across * side,
					zombie.y + 0.95,
					zombie.z - math.cos(zombie.yaw) * ahead - math.sin(zombie.yaw) * across * side
				),
				ui.turn(0.0, zombie.yaw, 0.0),
				ui.paint(skin)
			],
			0.16,
			0.16,
			0.62
		))
	}
	return pieces
}

// Green, and staying green: a shot one goes darker and duller rather than grey, so
// how much fight is left in it is something you can see without ever having to
// wonder what you are looking at. All three of these are dark for their colour,
// because the map is at night and a bright green zombie at midnight would be the
// one thing in the picture that was lit from nowhere.
//
// `Tone.RGBA` is what a computed colour is for, and it is worth saying why it is
// not `Tone.HEX` of a string built here: `HEX` is *checked*, so a colour that is
// not `#` and then hex digits comes back as `Normal` rather than as whatever was
// in the string. Which is the right behaviour — and the reason to hand the
// channels over as the numbers they already are.
func rot(health: Int): ui.Tone {
	gone := strength(Breed.Zombie()) - floored(health)
	fade := gone * 100 / strength(Breed.Zombie())
	return ui.Tone.RGBA(34 + fade / 4, 108 - fade * 2 / 5, 30 + fade / 5, 255)
}

// A merman, in the two colours a thing in cold water is: slate and a green that
// goes out of it as it is shot.
func scale(health: Int): ui.Tone {
	gone := strength(Breed.Merman()) - floored(health)
	fade := gone * 100 / strength(Breed.Merman())
	return ui.Tone.RGBA(26 + fade / 3, 92 - fade / 4, 104 - fade / 3, 255)
}

// A vampire: red-black, and paler as it goes — which runs the other way from the
// two above on purpose, because a vampire that has been drinking is *darker* again
// and that is the thing you want to be able to see.
func pallor(health: Int): ui.Tone {
	gone := strength(Breed.Vampire()) - floored(health)
	fade := gone * 100 / strength(Breed.Vampire())
	return ui.Tone.RGBA(76 + fade / 3, 20 + fade / 4, 30 + fade / 3, 255)
}

// A colour per player, computed from where they are. This is what `Tone.RGBA` is
// for: there is no role called "the other player", and no closed set could have
// one — the colour has to come from the value.
//
// It is the same on every screen, which matters more than it looks: "the green one
// shot me" has to mean the same thing on both machines. The port carries the
// weight because it is the part of an endpoint that actually differs between two
// players on one machine; `bytes` alone would give every 14-character endpoint the
// same colour, which is to say every player in a local game.
//
// The channels stay dark enough for the white name above them to read. A role
// brings a legible foreground with it; a colour you computed brings nothing, so
// the contrast is yours to get right.
func colour(endpoint: Str): ui.Tone {
	seed := portOf(endpoint) * 37 + bytes(endpoint) * 101
	return ui.Tone.RGBA(50 + seed % 130, 50 + seed * 7 % 140, 80 + seed * 13 % 120, 255)
}

// The chat, under the picture and over the footer. It is the same height whether
// anybody is talking or not, and that is deliberate: a strip that grew with the
// conversation would resize the picture — and move the crosshair in the middle of
// it — every time somebody said hello.
func chatter(model: Model): ui.View {
	mut ui.View[dyn] rows = []
	for each line in model.chat {
		append(rows, ui.row([ui.gap(8), ui.align(ui.Align.Center())], [
			ui.text([
				ui.background(colour(line.endpoint)),
				ui.tone(ui.Tone.HEX("#ffffff")),
				ui.size(ui.TextSize.Caption()),
				ui.pad(2)
			], " {line.who} "),
			ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Caption())], line.text)
		]))
	}
	append(rows, prompt(model))
	return ui.column([
		ui.height(124),
		ui.pad(6),
		ui.gap(2),
		// Newest at the bottom, which is what `Justify.End` says here, and the
		// oldest scrolls away rather than pushing the picture about.
		ui.justify(ui.Justify.End()),
		ui.scroll(ui.Axis.Vertical()),
		ui.background(ui.Tone.Muted())
	], rows)
}

// The line being typed, with a block where the next letter goes. There is nothing
// to focus and no cursor to blink: it is a `ui.text` of a `Str` in the model, and
// the keyboard is what writes into that.
func prompt(model: Model): ui.View {
	if model.composing == false {
		if len(model.chat) == 0 {
			return ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Caption()), ui.tone(ui.Tone.Muted())],
				"press t to say something")
		}
		return ui.none()
	}
	return ui.row([ui.gap(8), ui.align(ui.Align.Center())], [
		ui.icon([ui.tone(ui.Tone.Good())], ui.Icon.Reply()),
		ui.text([], "{model.saying}▌"),
		ui.spacer(),
		ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Caption()), ui.tone(ui.Tone.Muted())],
			"enter sends it · escape forgets it")
	])
}

func view(model: Model): ui.View {
	if model.screen is Screen.Lobby {
		return ui.column([ui.grow(1)], [lobby(model)])
	}
	return ui.column([ui.height(0), ui.grow(1)], [
		hud(model),
		ui.scene(
			[
				ui.grow(1),
				// The eye, and where it is looking. Both come straight out of the
				// state: the camera is not something this program owns, it is
				// something it describes.
				ui.eye(model.body.x, model.body.y + eyes(), model.body.z),
				ui.aim(model.body.yaw, model.body.pitch),
				ui.lens(78),
				// Night, and this is the whole of it: a sky the colour of a sky at
				// night, and fog that fades everything into it. There is no light to
				// turn down — the renderer's is fixed — so what makes it dark is the
				// palette, which is why every colour in `plan`'s districts is a
				// quarter of what it would be by day.
				//
				// Far enough to make out the tower's mast from the market, and not
				// far enough to see who is standing under it: a map worth learning is
				// a map you cannot see all of, and at night that comes for free.
				ui.fog(78),
				ui.background(ui.Tone.HEX("#070c15")),
				// The mouse is asked for while the game is being played and given
				// back the moment anything is in front of it.
				ui.grab(playing(model)),
				ui.crosshair(model.aiming && alive(model) && model.composing == false),
				ui.onFrame(Msg.Tick(_)),
				ui.onKeyDown(Msg.Pressed(_)),
				ui.onKeyUp(Msg.Released(_)),
				ui.onLook(Msg.Looked(_, _)),
				ui.onGrab(Msg.Grabbed(_))
			],
			world(model)
		),
		chatter(model),
		footer(model),
		panel(model)
	])
}

// Whether the mouse should be captured: playing, not reading the score, not
// waiting to come back.
func playing(model: Model): Bool {
	return model.board == false && model.screen is Screen.Arena
}

func hud(model: Model): ui.View {
	return ui.row([ui.pad(10), ui.gap(14), ui.align(ui.Align.Center())], [
		ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Subtitle()), ui.heading(1)], model.me),
		bar(model.body.health),
		ui.text([], "{model.body.health}"),
		ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Caption()), ui.tone(ui.Tone.Muted())],
			whereabouts(model)),
		lungs(model),
		ui.spacer(),
		ui.text([ui.tone(ui.Tone.Warn())], "{len(mob(model))} up"),
		ui.text([ui.tone(ui.Tone.Good())], "felled {model.felled}"),
		mode(model),
		ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Caption()), ui.tone(ui.Tone.Muted())],
			model.mine),
		ui.button([ui.on(Msg.Left()), ui.tone(ui.Tone.Danger())], "Leave")
	])
}

// What kind of game this is, which is not a setting anywhere: it is read off the
// roster every time the screen is drawn. Alone, there is nobody to shoot but the
// pack; with anybody else, there is.
func mode(model: Model): ui.View {
	if alone(model) {
		return ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Caption()), ui.tone(ui.Tone.Muted())],
			"alone · you and whatever is out there")
	}
	return ui.row([ui.gap(10), ui.align(ui.Align.Center())], [
		ui.text([ui.tone(ui.Tone.Good())], "kills {model.kills}"),
		ui.text([ui.tone(ui.Tone.Danger())], "deaths {model.deaths}"),
		ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Caption()), ui.tone(ui.Tone.Muted())],
			"{len(model.others) + 1} playing")
	])
}

// The breath, and only while it is going: a full one is not news. Eight cells and a
// word when there are none left — and the word is the part that matters, because by
// then you have a second or two to decide whether the far shore is nearer than the
// one behind you.
func lungs(model: Model): ui.View {
	if model.air >= breath() {
		return ui.none()
	}
	mut ui.View[dyn] cells = []
	for i := 0; i < 8; i++ {
		append(cells, ui.text(
			[ui.width(8), ui.height(12), ui.background(bubble(model.air, i))],
			" "
		))
	}
	return ui.row([ui.gap(6), ui.align(ui.Align.Center())], [
		ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Caption()), ui.tone(ui.Tone.Muted())], "air"),
		ui.row([ui.gap(2), ui.align(ui.Align.Center())], cells),
		gasping(model)
	])
}

func bubble(air: Int, cell: Int): ui.Tone {
	if air <= cell * breath() / 8 {
		return ui.Tone.HEX("#1d2630")
	}
	if air < breath() / 4 {
		return ui.Tone.Danger()
	}
	return ui.Tone.HEX("#4f8fd0")
}

func gasping(model: Model): ui.View {
	if model.air > 0 {
		return ui.none()
	}
	return ui.text([ui.tone(ui.Tone.Danger()), ui.size(ui.TextSize.Caption())],
		"drowning")
}

// Ten cells, however many of them are left. A bar is a row of small boxes, which
// is the sort of thing the widget set is meant to be enough for.
func bar(health: Int): ui.View {
	mut ui.View[dyn] cells = []
	for i := 0; i < 10; i++ {
		append(cells, ui.text(
			[ui.width(10), ui.height(12), ui.background(shade(health, i))],
			" "
		))
	}
	return ui.row([ui.gap(2), ui.align(ui.Align.Center())], cells)
}

func shade(health: Int, cell: Int): ui.Tone {
	if health <= cell * 10 {
		return ui.Tone.HEX("#2f343b")
	}
	if health <= 30 {
		return ui.Tone.Danger()
	}
	if health <= 60 {
		return ui.Tone.Warn()
	}
	return ui.Tone.Good()
}

func footer(model: Model): ui.View {
	return ui.row([ui.pad(8), ui.gap(12), ui.align(ui.Align.Center())], [
		ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Caption()), ui.tone(ui.Tone.Muted())],
			"W A S D walk · space jumps and swims up · ctrl dives · mouse looks · left button shoots · t talks · tab for the score · escape to let the mouse go"),
		ui.spacer(),
		trouble(model.trouble)
	])
}

func trouble(why: Str): ui.View {
	if why == "" {
		return ui.none()
	}
	return ui.text([ui.tone(ui.Tone.Danger()), ui.size(ui.TextSize.Caption())], why)
}

// Whatever is in front of the game right now: the score, the two seconds after
// being shot, or nothing. An overlay is the widget for exactly this, and it is
// what also takes the mouse back — the picture behind it keeps being drawn.
func panel(model: Model): ui.View {
	if model.board {
		return ui.overlay([ui.pad(20), ui.onDismiss(Msg.Grabbed(true))], scores(model))
	}
	if alive(model) == false {
		return ui.overlay([ui.pad(28)], ui.column([ui.gap(8), ui.align(ui.Align.Center())], [
			ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Title()), ui.tone(ui.Tone.Danger())], "You went down"),
			ui.text([ui.tone(ui.Tone.Muted())], "back in {seconds(model.down)}")
		]))
	}
	if model.aiming == false {
		return ui.overlay([ui.pad(24)], ui.column([ui.gap(6), ui.align(ui.Align.Center())], [
			ui.icon([ui.tone(ui.Tone.Good())], ui.Icon.Star()),
			ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Subtitle())], "Click to play"),
			ui.text([ui.tone(ui.Tone.Muted()), ui.size(ui.TextSize.Caption())],
				"escape gives the mouse back")
		]))
	}
	return ui.none()
}

// Whole seconds only. The panel is redrawn whenever it changes, and a countdown
// in milliseconds would be a new document sixty times a second.
func seconds(ms: Int): Str {
	return "{ms / 1000 + 1}s"
}

func scores(model: Model): ui.View {
	mut ui.View[dyn] rows = [
		ui.row([ui.gap(12)], [
			ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Subtitle())], "{model.me} (you)"),
			ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Caption())], whereabouts(model)),
			ui.spacer(),
			ui.text([ui.tone(ui.Tone.Good())], "{model.kills}"),
			ui.text([ui.tone(ui.Tone.Muted())], "{model.deaths}")
		])
	]
	for each foe in model.others {
		append(rows, ui.row([ui.gap(12)], [
			ui.text([ui.background(colour(foe.endpoint)),
				ui.tone(ui.Tone.HEX("#ffffff")), ui.pad(2)], " {foe.who} "),
			// Where they were when they last said. On a place this size that is
			// the difference between a score board and a way of finding anybody:
			// it costs nothing to work out, because their position is already here.
			ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Caption())],
				district(plan(model.seed), foe.x, foe.y, foe.z)),
			ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Caption()), ui.tone(ui.Tone.Muted())],
				foe.endpoint),
			ui.spacer(),
			ui.text([ui.tone(ui.Tone.Good())], "{foe.score}")
		]))
	}
	append(rows, ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Caption()), ui.tone(ui.Tone.Muted())],
		"tab closes this"))
	return ui.column([ui.gap(8), ui.width(420)], rows)
}

func lobby(model: Model): ui.View {
	return ui.column([ui.pad(32), ui.gap(12), ui.width(470)], [
		ui.row([ui.gap(8), ui.align(ui.Align.Center())], [
			ui.icon([ui.tone(ui.Tone.Good())], ui.Icon.Star()),
			ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Title()), ui.heading(1)], "Hive Arena")
		]),
		ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Caption()), ui.tone(ui.Tone.Muted())], "Your name"),
		ui.input([ui.onInput(Msg.NameTyped(_)), ui.onSubmit(joinedOf),
			ui.placeholder("Ada")], model.me),
		ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Caption()), ui.tone(ui.Tone.Muted())],
			"Somebody already playing"),
		ui.input([ui.onInput(Msg.PeerTyped(_)), ui.onSubmit(joinedOf),
			ui.placeholder("127.0.0.1:9201")], model.peer),
		ui.text([ui.size(ui.TextSize.Caption()), ui.tone(ui.Tone.Muted())],
			"This node is listening on {model.mine}. Anybody they have met, you meet."),
		ui.row([ui.gap(8), ui.align(ui.Align.Center())], [
			trouble(model.trouble),
			ui.spacer(),
			// Alone needs nobody's address, and it is not a different game: this
			// node is still listening, so anybody who turns up joins the one you
			// are already playing.
			ui.button([ui.on(Msg.Alone()), ui.disabled(usable(model.me) == false)],
				"Play alone"),
			ui.button([ui.on(Msg.Joined()), ui.tone(ui.Tone.Good()),
				ui.disabled(ready(model) == false)], "Join somebody")
		])
	])
}

// `onSubmit` hands back what was typed and neither of these cares: it is already
// in the model.
func joinedOf(_typed: Str): Msg {
	return Msg.Joined()
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The fold
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The window's mailbox and the node's. A key, a mouse, a frame and another
// machine all arrive here, and there is one `is` chain for the lot — which is the
// claim `hive.syslink` makes, holding as far as the keyboard.
//
// There is no mutex in this program. The fold is the mutex.
proc update(model: Model, msg: Msg, from: hive.syslink.Envelope): Model {
	mut Model next = model

	if msg is Msg.Tick(ms) {
		return ticked(model, ms)
	}
	if msg is Msg.Looked(dx, dy) {
		// Not while there is a sentence on the go: the picture holds still, which
		// is what makes reading back what you wrote possible.
		if alive(model) && model.composing == false {
			next.body = turned(model, dx, dy)
		}
		return next
	}
	if msg is Msg.Pressed(key) {
		// The one branch that decides whether a key is a letter or a move, and it
		// is checked first because while it is true nothing else about the key
		// matters. `enter` is the only key here that acts, so it is the only one
		// that leaves this `func`-shaped corner of the fold.
		if model.composing {
			if key == "enter" {
				return say(sealed(model), model.saying)
			}
			if key == "escape" {
				return sealed(model)
			}
			return typed(model, key)
		}
		if key == "t" {
			return opening(model)
		}
		if key == "tab" {
			next.board = model.board == false
			return next
		}
		if held(model, key) == false {
			append(next.held, key)
		}
		return next
	}
	if msg is Msg.Released(key) {
		// Nothing is held while typing, so there is nothing to let go of — and the
		// key that comes up is a letter that has already been typed.
		if model.composing {
			return model
		}
		next.held = filter(model.held, isNotKey(_, key))
		return next
	}
	if msg is Msg.Grabbed(on) {
		next.aiming = on
		// Letting the mouse go is how you get out, so it closes the score too —
		// otherwise escape would leave the panel up with nothing holding it.
		if on {
			next.board = false
		}
		// The mouse going takes the half-typed line with it. Escape is one key and
		// it means both things, and this is where the second one happens: the
		// browser lets the pointer go and tells us about it afterwards.
		if on == false {
			return sealed(next)
		}
		return next
	}
	if msg is Msg.NameTyped(text) {
		next.me = text
		return next
	}
	if msg is Msg.PeerTyped(text) {
		next.peer = text
		return next
	}
	if msg is Msg.Joined {
		return joined(model, from)
	}
	if msg is Msg.Alone {
		// The same start, minus the knocking. The window is still published and
		// the node is still listening, which is the whole reason this is not a
		// separate mode: somebody knocking turns it into a multiplayer game
		// without this program doing anything about it.
		return arrived(model, from)
	}
	if msg is Msg.Left {
		return leaving(model)
	}

	// --- from the other machines ---

	// The one message anybody waits for an answer to, and this is the branch that
	// answers it. A service replies with one of its *own* messages, so the reply
	// type is the mailbox type and nothing is annotated.
	if msg is Msg.Hello(endpoint, who, seed) {
		hive.syslink.answer(from, Msg.Here(model.mine, model.me, roster(model),
			model.pack, model.seed))
		mut Model greeted = settled(met(model, endpoint, who), endpoint, seed)
		greeted.nodes = hive.syslink.peers()
		// Somebody being here answers "is anybody there?" as well as a reply would
		// have. A node that was unreachable when we knocked and has since knocked
		// back is not a problem any more, and leaving the warning up would be
		// telling the player something that stopped being true.
		greeted.trouble = ""
		return greeted
	}
	if msg is Msg.Met(endpoint, who, heard, theirs, seed) {
		// Their world first, because everything else in this branch is about where
		// things are and that is the line that decides where anything is.
		mut Model known = told(settled(met(model, endpoint, who), endpoint, seed),
			endpoint, theirs)
		// Everybody they know, greeted in turn. This is the line that turns a
		// chain of introductions into a mesh — and it terminates because only
		// strangers are greeted, and greeting one makes them known.
		introduce(known, strangers(known, heard), hive.syslink.self(from))
		known.nodes = hive.syslink.peers()
		known.trouble = ""
		return known
	}
	if msg is Msg.Moved {
		return moved(model, msg)
	}
	if msg is Msg.Saw(ax, ay, az, bx, by, bz) {
		append(next.tracers, Tracer(ax, ay, az, bx, by, bz, 120))
		return next
	}
	if msg is Msg.Hit(endpoint, who, hurt) {
		mut Model bleeding = wounded(model, hurt)
		// Going down is mine to report, and the credit goes back to whoever did
		// it. Nobody else can honestly say this happened.
		if alive(model) && alive(bleeding) == false {
			far := hive.syslink.on(endpoint, #Arena)
			async far(Msg.Killed(model.mine, model.me))
		}
		return bleeding
	}
	if msg is Msg.Killed(endpoint, who) {
		next.kills = model.kills + 1
		next.others = map(model.others, credited(_, endpoint))
		return next
	}
	if msg is Msg.Said(endpoint, who, text) {
		return spoke(model, endpoint, who, text)
	}
	// --- the dead ---

	if msg is Msg.Pack(endpoint, theirs) {
		return told(model, endpoint, theirs)
	}
	// Somebody shot one of mine. Only this node can say what that does, because
	// only this node is moving them.
	if msg is Msg.Clawed(endpoint, id, hurt) {
		if finished(model, id, hurt) {
			hunter := hive.syslink.on(endpoint, #Arena)
			async hunter(Msg.Felled())
		}
		return clawed(model, id, hurt)
	}
	if msg is Msg.Bitten(hurt) {
		return wounded(model, hurt)
	}
	if msg is Msg.Felled {
		next.felled = model.felled + 1
		return next
	}

	if msg is Msg.Gone(endpoint) {
		mut Model without = parted(model, endpoint)
		// Their pack goes with them: nothing else was ever pretending to know
		// where those were, so leaving them on the screen would be drawing a
		// simulation that has stopped.
		without.swarm = filter(model.swarm, notOwnedBy(_, endpoint))
		without.nodes = hive.syslink.peers()
		return without
	}
	if msg is Msg.Unreachable(endpoint) {
		next.trouble = "No answer from {endpoint}. Is it running?"
		return next
	}
	// `Here` only ever comes back as the answer to a `Hello`, never into the
	// mailbox — but it is one of the mailbox's messages, so it has a branch.
	return model
}

func isNotKey(had: Str, key: Str): Bool {
	return had != key
}

// Whose world this is.
//
// Two nodes that meet have to agree on the seed, and there is nobody to ask — so
// they decide it from the one thing both of them already know: their own endpoints.
// The lower one's world wins. That is the same tie-break `hive.syslink` uses when
// two nodes dial each other in the same instant, and it is chosen for the same
// reason: it is the only answer both ends work out alike, without a round trip and
// without a referee.
//
// It settles the awkward cases as well as the ordinary one. Two people pressing
// Join at the same moment cannot end up in two different maps, because each of them
// applies this rule and they both get the same answer. And as the introductions
// spread — everybody meets everybody, see `Met` — every node in the mesh converges
// on the lowest endpoint's world, however late that node turned up.
//
// Adopting one is a rebuild: the body goes back to its spot in the new map, because
// where it was standing in the old one may be inside a wall in this one, and both
// packs go, because they were raised in a world that has stopped existing.
func settled(model: Model, endpoint: Str, seed: Int): Model {
	if seed == model.seed || endpoint > model.mine {
		return model
	}
	mut Model next = model
	next.seed = seed
	next.body = fresh(mySpot(model.mine, seed))
	next.air = breath()
	next.pack = noone()
	next.swarm = noone()
	next.trouble = ""
	return next
}

// One frame: step the world and this node's own pack, fire if the trigger is
// down, and now and then tell everybody what both are doing.
proc ticked(model: Model, ms: Int): Model {
	mut Model next = stepped(model, ms)

	// This node's own pack. They are stepped here — on the fold's thread, in the
	// same turn as everything else — so a frame is one consistent picture: the
	// player has moved, the pack has moved, and nothing was half way.
	assault := stirred(model, ms)
	next.pack = assault.horde
	next = waiting(next, ms)
	// Each breed on its own books. Two of them may be owed one in the same frame
	// and each is asked separately, which is the whole of what "separately" means:
	// there is no one number here and no one clock.
	for each breed in due(next) {
		next = raised(next, breed, somewhere(next, breed))
	}
	for each breed in filled(next) {
		next = carrying(next, breed, roll(breed))
	}
	// A bite carries its own damage, because a merman's grab and a zombie's are not
	// worth the same and the wound is applied by whoever was bitten.
	for each bite in assault.bitten {
		if bite.endpoint == model.mine {
			next = wounded(next, bite.damage)
		}
		if bite.endpoint != model.mine {
			far := hive.syslink.on(bite.endpoint, #Arena)
			async far(Msg.Bitten(bite.damage))
		}
	}

	if held(model, "mouse1") && model.cooldown <= 0 && alive(model) {
		shot := aimed(model)
		next.cooldown = 190
		append(next.tracers, tracing(shot))
		next = fire(next, shot)
	}

	// Fifteen times a second rather than sixty. The others draw this player and
	// this pack from the last thing they heard, and hearing it every four frames
	// is enough to look continuous — while sending sixty would be four times the
	// traffic for a difference nobody can see.
	if next.beat <= 0 {
		next.beat = 66
		announce(next)
	}
	return next
}

// The tracer goes to everybody so that every screen sees the same shot. What was
// hit goes to exactly one node — the player who was hit, or whoever owns the
// zombie that was — because that is the node entitled to say what it did.
//
// A zombie of this node's own is the one case that answers itself: no message, no
// waiting, and the score goes up in the same turn.
proc fire(model: Model, shot: Shot): Model {
	for each foe in model.others {
		far := hive.syslink.on(foe.endpoint, #Arena)
		async far(Msg.Saw(shot.ax, shot.ay, shot.az, shot.bx, shot.by, shot.bz))
	}
	if shot.at is Struck.Player(endpoint) {
		hurt := hive.syslink.on(endpoint, #Arena)
		async hurt(Msg.Hit(model.mine, model.me, damage()))
	}
	if shot.at is Struck.Undead(owner, id) {
		if owner == model.mine {
			mut Model mine = model
			if finished(model, id, damage()) {
				mine.felled = model.felled + 1
			}
			return clawed(mine, id, damage())
		}
		theirs := hive.syslink.on(owner, #Arena)
		async theirs(Msg.Clawed(model.mine, id, damage()))
	}
	return model
}

// A line to everybody, and onto this screen.
//
// It goes out on the connections that are **already open**: `hive.syslink.on` is a
// name for a service on a node, and the node has one connection, filed under the
// endpoint it advertised — so this is one more message down the same TLS pipe the
// positions and the packs are going down, and there is no chat server, no second
// port and no second socket anywhere. `async` again, for the reason a position is:
// a line nobody can be told is not worth waiting to find out about.
//
// Nobody sends to themselves — the roster is everybody *else* — so the last line
// is where this node's own words land on its own screen.
proc say(model: Model, text: Str): Model {
	// The same rule that decides a name is worth joining under decides a line is
	// worth sending: something other than spaces.
	if usable(text) == false {
		return model
	}
	said := clipped(text)
	for each foe in model.others {
		far := hive.syslink.on(foe.endpoint, #Arena)
		async far(Msg.Said(model.mine, model.me, said))
	}
	return spoke(model, model.mine, model.me, said)
}

// A number between one and however many of that breed a node may carry, which is
// what decides how many of them it is carrying tonight. `hive.crypto` is where the
// randomness comes from — the same call a token is made with — and `roll` is a
// `proc` because finding out is *doing* something: a `func` handed the same state
// twice has to answer the same way twice, and this one must not.
proc roll(breed: Breed): Int {
	return 1 + hexOf(hive.crypto.randomHex(2)) % plenty(breed)
}

// The world's number, rolled once by whoever starts a game. Six hex digits is
// sixteen million maps, which is enough that nobody will see the same one twice —
// and it is *this* call, and only this one, that the whole map is downstream of.
// Everything after it is arithmetic (see `hash`), which is what lets the number
// travel instead of the map.
proc rolled(): Int {
	return hexOf(hive.crypto.randomHex(6))
}

// Somewhere in the arena to raise one, which is a guess checked against `decent`
// rather than a place worked out — the same way you would find a free seat in a
// room. Sixteen guesses rather than a dozen, because the room got bigger and most
// of it is now further from the living than one of the dead is any use at: a guess
// anywhere in a hundred metres lands inside `earshot` about a third of the time,
// and sixteen of them miss together a couple of times in a thousand raisings. When
// they do, the emptiest spot is a better answer than a seventeenth guess.
proc somewhere(model: Model, breed: Breed): Spot {
	for i := 0; i < 16; i++ {
		place := guess(model, breed)
		if decent(model, breed, place.x, place.z) {
			return place
		}
	}
	return unfit(model, breed)
}

// Where to try. A merman is guessed inside the lake rather than inside the map,
// because the lake is a twentieth of the map and sixteen guesses at the whole thing
// would put one in the water about as often as it rains.
proc guess(model: Model, breed: Breed): Spot {
	if breed == Breed.Merman() {
		pool := plan(model.seed).lake
		return Spot(pool.x + adrift(pool.w), pool.z + adrift(pool.d), 0.0)
	}
	return Spot(anywhere(), anywhere(), 0.0)
}

// A coordinate anywhere between the walls, to the centimetre.
proc anywhere(): Float {
	return conv.itf(hexOf(hive.crypto.randomHex(4)) % 10400) / 100.0 - 52.0
}

// And one anywhere across something `span` wide, never quite at the edge of it.
proc adrift(span: Float): Float {
	room := conv.round(span * 100.0) - 300
	if room < 1 {
		return 0.0
	}
	return conv.itf(hexOf(hive.crypto.randomHex(4)) % room) / 100.0 - span / 2.0 + 1.5
}

// When sixteen guesses have all landed somewhere unfit. A merman goes to the middle
// of the lake, which is water whatever the seed said; everything else goes to the
// emptiest spot, which is always ground.
func unfit(model: Model, breed: Breed): Spot {
	if breed == Breed.Merman() {
		pool := plan(model.seed).lake
		return Spot(pool.x, pool.z, 0.0)
	}
	return emptiest(model)
}

// Hex digits into the number they spell. `hive.conv.sti` reads base ten, and this
// is base sixteen — four lines, and the whole of what a `randomHex` needs to
// become a number.
func hexOf(text: Str): Int {
	mut Int total = 0
	for each digit in split(text, "") {
		if indexOf("0123456789abcdef", digit) is Result.Ok(value) {
			total = total * 16 + value
		}
	}
	return total
}

// Where this player is and what its pack is doing, to everybody who might be
// drawing either. `async` is the send that waits for nothing and cannot fail —
// which is what a position update wants to be: the next one is along in 66
// milliseconds, and a lost one is not worth reporting, let alone waiting for.
proc announce(model: Model): void {
	for each foe in model.others {
		far := hive.syslink.on(foe.endpoint, #Arena)
		async far(Msg.Moved(model.mine, model.me, model.body.x, model.body.y,
			model.body.z, model.body.yaw, model.body.health))
		async far(Msg.Pack(model.mine, model.pack))
	}
}

// Joining is walking in and then going knocking.
proc joined(model: Model, from: hive.syslink.Envelope): Model {
	mut Model next = arrived(model, from)
	if next.screen is Screen.Lobby {
		return next
	}
	// Off the fold's thread. A greeting is a *waited* call and the fold must not
	// wait: a screen that stops drawing while it dials is the one thing a game may
	// never do.
	async greet(model.peer, model.mine, model.me, next.seed, hive.syslink.self(from))
	return next
}

// Walking into the arena, which is the same thing whether anybody else is in it.
// The registration is what makes this screen addressable from another machine, and
// it happens here rather than in `main` because a service's address does not exist
// until the service is running — inside the fold is the first place there is one.
proc arrived(model: Model, from: hive.syslink.Envelope): Model {
	mut Model next = model
	if hive.syslink.register(#Arena, hive.syslink.self(from)) is Result.Error(problem) {
		if problem.reason != "Taken" {
			next.trouble = "Could not publish this window — {problem.message}"
			return next
		}
	}
	next.screen = Screen.Arena()
	// A world, rolled here and nowhere else. A node that is about to go knocking
	// still needs one: it has to stand somewhere while it waits for an answer, and
	// if the answer comes back with a lower endpoint on it this map lasted a second
	// (see `settled`).
	next.seed = rolled()
	next.body = fresh(mySpot(model.mine, next.seed))
	next.air = breath()
	next.trouble = ""
	next.nodes = hive.syslink.peers()
	// How many of each this node is carrying tonight — three rolls, three clocks.
	// The first zombie is along within a second, since the pack starts empty and
	// wants filling; the lake takes a little longer to notice.
	next.broods = broods()
	for each breed in kinds() {
		next = carrying(next, breed, roll(breed))
	}
	return next
}

// Everybody a peer told us about, greeted the same way we greeted the peer.
proc introduce(model: Model, fresh: Str[], window: hive.syslink.Address): void {
	for each endpoint in fresh {
		async greet(endpoint, model.mine, model.me, model.seed, window)
	}
}

// "Is anybody there?", asked properly — and the one call in the program that
// waits for an answer, so the one that can report a failure. It retries because
// two nodes started a second apart would otherwise knock at a window that has not
// been published yet, and a knock is safe to repeat: `met` renames an endpoint it
// already knows rather than adding it twice.
proc greet(peer: Str, mine: Str, me: Str, seed: Int, window: hive.syslink.Address): void {
	for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
		far := hive.syslink.on(peer, #Arena)
		answer := far(Msg.Hello(mine, me, seed)) with timeout 2000
		if answer is Result.Ok(reply) {
			if reply is Msg.Here(endpoint, who, roster, horde, theirs) {
				async window(Msg.Met(endpoint, who, roster, horde, theirs))
				return
			}
		}
		hive.task.sleep(400)
	}
	async window(Msg.Unreachable(peer))
}

proc leaving(model: Model): Model {
	for each foe in model.others {
		far := hive.syslink.on(foe.endpoint, #Arena)
		async far(Msg.Gone(model.mine))
	}
	mut Model next = model
	next.screen = Screen.Lobby()
	next.others = nobody()
	next.tracers = notracers()
	// Both packs go: this node's own because it has stopped stepping them, and
	// everybody else's because it has stopped listening. The books go back to
	// nothing too — the next game is a different world with a different seed, and
	// nothing about this one is worth carrying into it.
	next.pack = noone()
	next.swarm = noone()
	next.broods = broods()
	next.air = breath()
	next.held = nothing()
	next.aiming = false
	next.board = false
	// What was said goes too. A chat is what the people here are saying, and there
	// is nobody here.
	next.chat = unsaid()
	next.composing = false
	next.saying = ""
	return next
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

func nobody(): Foe[dyn] {
	mut Foe[dyn] none = []
	return none
}

func notracers(): Tracer[dyn] {
	mut Tracer[dyn] none = []
	return none
}

func unsaid(): Line[dyn] {
	mut Line[dyn] none = []
	return none
}

func noone(): Fiend[dyn] {
	mut Fiend[dyn] none = []
	return none
}

func nothing(): Str[dyn] {
	mut Str[dyn] none = []
	return none
}

func nowhere(): Str[dyn] {
	mut Str[dyn] none = []
	return none
}

// The lobby's model. The seed is nothing yet: there is no world until somebody
// presses a button, and which world it is depends on which button (see `arrived`
// and `settled`).
func start(mine: Str, peer: Str): Model {
	return Model(
		"",
		mine,
		peer,
		Screen.Lobby(),
		false,
		"",
		0,
		fresh(spot(0, 0)),
		nothing(),
		nobody(),
		notracers(),
		unsaid(),
		noone(),
		noone(),
		broods(),
		0,
		0,
		0,
		0,
		0,
		breath(),
		0,
		0,
		0,
		false,
		false,
		"",
		nowhere()
	)
}

// The obvious guess for somebody to join through, so two terminals on one machine
// need no typing: this same machine, on the other port of the pair. It is built
// out of wherever this node actually is rather than from an address written down
// here, so the guess is right on a laptop, in a container and on somebody else's
// network.
func facing(mine: Str): Str {
	port := portOf(mine)
	if port == 9200 {
		return "{hostOf(mine)}:9201"
	}
	if port == 9201 {
		return "{hostOf(mine)}:9200"
	}
	return ""
}

// The host half of an "ip:port". Its twin is `portOf`, and the two of them are the
// whole of what this program does to an endpoint — which is the point of an
// endpoint being a `Str`: it is deployment data, and string work is all it takes.
func hostOf(endpoint: Str): Str {
	parts := split(endpoint, ":")
	if parts bounds 0 {
		return parts[0]
	}
	return endpoint
}

// Where this node tells peers to dial it — **asked of the machine, not written
// down**. A node has no name in `hive.syslink`; it is identified by where it is,
// so this endpoint is the one thing about the program that has to be true.
//
// `hive.net.localAddress` is the answer to exactly that question: the source
// address the operating system would stamp on a packet leaving by the default
// route, which is the right one on a machine with several interfaces. Loopback is
// deliberately *not* something it answers with — a machine holding only
// `127.0.0.1` has no address a peer could dial, and it says so rather than handing
// back one that works until the second player turns out to be on another host.
// That case is the one place below where the loopback address is the right answer
// rather than a lazy one: two terminals on a machine with its networking down can
// still play each other.
proc hereOn(port: Int): Str {
	if hive.net.localAddress() is Result.Ok(address) {
		return "{address}:{port}"
	}
	return "127.0.0.1:{port}"
}

// What the command line asked for. A bare port is a port on this machine's own
// address; anything with a colon in it is taken as the endpoint itself, which is
// what a node behind a router — or on a chosen interface — needs to advertise.
proc advertised(given: Str): Str {
	if indexOf(given, ":") is Result.Ok(_) {
		return given
	}
	if conv.sti(given) is Result.Ok(port) {
		return hereOn(port)
	}
	return hereOn(9200)
}

proc main(): void {
	args := hive.term.args()
	mut Str mine = hereOn(9200)
	if args bounds 0 {
		mine = advertised(args[0])
	}
	// A second argument fills the lobby's "somebody already playing" field in
	// advance, which is what a fourth terminal wants — and what a script driving
	// the game wants, since it is then the only thing that differs between nodes.
	// It is a convenience and nothing more: the field is still there, still
	// editable, and joining still goes through it.
	mut Str through = facing(mine)
	if args bounds 1 {
		through = advertised(args[1])
	}

	// The node starts accepting before the window opens. A node has no name — it
	// is identified by where it is — so this endpoint is ordinary runtime data.
	if hive.syslink.listen(mine) is Result.Error(problem) {
		panic problem
	}
	echo "listening on {mine}"

	// Opens a real application window: an installed Chrome or Edge in app mode,
	// and an ordinary tab where there is none. three.js was fetched once when this
	// program was first compiled and is inside the executable, so this runs with
	// no network and nothing installed.
	ui.window("Hive Arena — {mine}", view, update, start(mine, through))
}
